- op: discuss  # f4c2
  at: '2026-08-09T10:49:05Z'  # f4c2
  by: agent  # f4c2
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f4c2
  clock: 13  # f4c2
  nonce: f4c2  # f4c2
  message: "MECHANISM C LANDED (PR #253, dev 643608f95e) -- the first receiver-typing increment\nmeasured through a PAIRED TAINT A/B rather than through edges.\n\n    io boundary chains   3,977 -> 4,108   (+131)   chains LOST: 0\n    taint findings          22 ->    22   ( +0)\n    sanitized_flows          0 ->     0   ( +0)    verdicts changed: none\n\n  4 repos, both arms run on the same tree in the same session (so the delta is\n  attributable, not inherited from an older baseline): meson +90, poetry +19,\n  mitmproxy +16, kserve +6. fs_read 93 / fs_write 38.\n\nWHY THE TAINT ROW IS THE HEADLINE. This class's own direction rule says an emission\nor typing change must state which way it moves the walk's False verdict, because\nsince PR #214 a False earns `sanitized` and DROPS a flow. Receiver typing is what\narms the barrier arm (established on this thread, with the positive control two\nearlier accounts lacked). So the measurable risk here was SUPPRESSION. It did not\nfire: zero sanitized flows in either arm, zero findings moved, no verdict changed,\nand zero chains lost.\n\nWHAT SHIPPED, beyond the table row: an UNBOUND bare constructor name is no longer\ntrusted unless it is a real builtin. The old branch reasoned \"for a bare name that\nmeans the builtin\", true of `open` and false of `Path`; 254 corpus sites have a\nconstructor name that is also an in-repo class name, and trusting those mints an\nfs_write boundary AND a host_fs taint sink for any class merely named Path. This\ntightens INV-kipor's check from \"not contradicted\" to \"confirmed\".\n\nAlso: a constructor call is now a valid DERIVATION ROOT (82 of 294 assign-from-ctor\nsites = 28%), so PR #247's allowlist propagation finally has a root to start from\non this shape. The allowlist still gates which members propagate.\n\nEVIDENCE QUALITY, stated rather than implied: 3 of 3 sampled new chains adjudicated\nagainst source as genuine. That is a SPOT CHECK, not a precision figure -- there is\nstill no analogue here of the 25.9% / 85.7% numbers TYPE_PRESERVING_MEMBERS was\npriced against. Product/test split 91/40 (69.5%/30.5%), all test-path chains in\nmeson; a first pass said 100% product and was WRONG, because classify_test_file\ndoes not recognise meson's `unittests/` and `test cases/` naming.\n\nSTILL OPEN UNDER THIS CLASS, and NOT closed by this PR:\n  * the UNTYPED bare-Name population at large -- this addressed the\n    assign-from-external-constructor slice only.\n  * mechanism D (self.<attr> typed from __init__): ~344 reaches, but measured that\n    roughly HALF the sites need a new EMISSION branch as well, because the typed-emit\n    path is gated on a bare ast.Name receiver. Do not schedule it on the reach number\n    alone.\n  * mechanism E (unannotated parameter from a single call site): 220 reaches, but\n    205 of them in 3 repos and 202 on one pair (file.write). Not a corpus rate.\n  * mechanism B (in-repo class constructor): measured ZERO reaches. Do not build.\n  * mechanism A (in-repo factory return annotation): 27-53 reaches against the\n    largest FP exposure of any mechanism. Do not build standalone.\n  * the INLINE receiver form of a constructor root (`Path(x).write_text(y)`) is\n    deliberately NOT covered -- it lives in _process_call, a different scope from the\n    constructor resolver, and belongs with the ctor-root emission item.\n\nNOT MEASURED: precision at scale; any repo outside the four A/B'd; whether the taint\nneutrality above holds on a corpus where the barrier arm is reachable at all (on\nthese four it is not, so \"no suppression\" is a weaker statement than it looks)."  # f4c2
- op: discuss  # f34c
  at: '2026-08-09T23:08:57Z'  # f34c
  by: agent  # f34c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f34c
  clock: 14  # f34c
  nonce: f34c  # f34c
  message: "L2+L3 CLOSED FOR ONE PYTHON SHAPE (constructor-result root), PR #254. CLASS STAYS\nVIOLATED -- this is one instance shape in one language, not the invariant.\n\nThis item's chain is L1 catalogue -> L2 emission -> L3 receiver evidence -> L4 the F3\ngate, and its own root_cause records the load-bearing objection that \"an emission-only\nfix cannot move findings\". PR #254 is the first change in this family to clear L2 and L3\nTOGETHER, and it is the first to be measured at L4 rather than at the analyzer:\n\n  +125 boundary chains, +260 typed call edges, +260 total edges (every one net-new,\n  zero re-keyed), across pretix / sqlalchemy / mitmproxy / poetry / pyright / meson /\n  kserve. Positive control printed first (0 -> 1 boundary on a known-positive fixture).\n  Instrument in-repo: scripts/measure-ctor-root-typing-ab.py.\n\nThat is the contrast this class was filed to draw. PR #231 shipped an L2-only fix to\nINV-mumov and moved ZERO findings; this one carries the receiver TYPE into the module\nslot at the same time and moves 125. The class's prediction -- that a fix scoped to L2\nalone would be measured as a win at the analyzer and produce zero new findings -- is now\ncorroborated from the other side as well.\n\nCHOKEPOINT QUESTION, which the class exists to answer and which this instance answers\nfor python: the declining branches DID share one shape. The answer to \"what external\ntype does this receiver expression have\" was being computed privately inside\n_preserved_receiver_type, so the emission site could only ask the narrower \"what does\nthis DERIVATION preserve\" question, and a chain rooted at a constructor -- not a\nderivation of anything -- matched no branch. It is now ONE predicate (_receiver_type)\nconsumed by both callers, with a BEHAVIOURAL parity test (inline form vs assigned form\nmust agree on type and on boundary count for every shape, including the negatives) --\nnot a grep-for-the-call test, which a third copy that merely looks right would satisfy.\n_external_constructor_type moved to module level so the emission site inherits the\nINV-kipor binding check rather than re-deciding it; a local `class Path` and a\n`from decoy_lib import Path` are refused at a chain root exactly as at an assignment.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES NOT TOUCH, so the class is not read as narrowing more than it did:\nkotlin (WI-nasuf, no unresolved method-edge fallback at all), javascript, WI-makij, and\nINV-fokik / INV-fazim. No claim is made about any of them from this python result --\nnever reason by analogy across languages is a standing rule in this area and go already\nneeded neither #246 nor #247.\n\nSCOPE OF THE PYTHON WIN, stated because concentration limits it: 269 of 27,354\ncall-result-root sites in the cohort are typeable (~1%), and meson alone supplies 89 of\nthe 125 chains (71.2%). This is not an evenly distributed win."  # f34c
- op: discuss  # 6ccf
  at: '2026-08-10T02:05:11Z'  # 6ccf
  by: agent  # 6ccf
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 6ccf
  clock: 15  # 6ccf
  nonce: 6ccf  # 6ccf
  message: "GO MEASURED, AND ITS FAILURE SHAPE IS THE MIRROR OF PYTHON'S -- not the same defect\n(PR #254 / #256 session). Recorded because this class's whole premise is that per-analyzer\nbranches may share one shape, and here they demonstrably do NOT.\n\nMEASURED with a passing positive control, on os/exec.Cmd (a method-kind catalogue row\nunder the subprocess boundary, chosen because go's fs surface is almost entirely\nFUNCTION-kind -- os.Open / os.ReadFile / os.Create -- so python's pathlib shape has no go\nanalogue at all):\n\n  inline    exec.Command(\"sh\",\"-c\",x).Run()          -> .Run() typed to os/exec, 2 boundaries\n  assigned  cmd := exec.Command(...); cmd.Run()      -> go:external:0-0:Run:unresolved, 1\n\nPython was broken INLINE and worked ASSIGNED. Go is the reverse. A python-shaped fix\nwould not have transferred, which is the concrete case for the plan's\ntwo-languages-minimum rule rather than an argument for it.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS CLASS: do not generalise #254's fix to go. The go gap is receiver\ntyping through an ASSIGNMENT, a different branch from the emission-site root typing that\n#254 closed for python. Unfiled as a separate item for now because its blast radius is\nunmeasured -- the shapes where it bites are the method-kind go rows (net.Conn.Write,\nnet/http.Client.Do, os/exec.Cmd.*), and for exec.Cmd the boundary is still caught via the\ncatalogued exec.Command call itself, so the loss is partial. SIZE IT BEFORE BUILDING IT.\n\nA PROBE THAT MEASURED NOTHING, recorded so its zero is not later cited: I tried to isolate\nthe assigned-vs-inline gap on net/http.Client.Do (constructor is a composite literal, so\nthe Do row is the only one that can fire). BOTH arms returned 0 boundaries. Its positive\ncontrol therefore FAILED, so it distinguishes nothing and no conclusion is drawn from it.\nWhether net/http.Client.Do is reachable at all in go is OPEN and unmeasured."  # 6ccf
- op: discuss  # 3252
  at: '2026-08-10T02:14:04Z'  # 3252
  by: agent  # 3252
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 3252
  clock: 16  # 3252
  nonce: '3252'  # 3252
  message: "GO ASSIGNMENT GAP SIZED: 6 SITES ACROSS 9 REPOS. DO NOT BUILD IT.\n\nInstrument in-repo at scripts/measure-go-receiver-typing-gap.py, positive-controlled\nbefore use (a synthetic fixture with four known assigned sites; the control caught a real\nscanner bug first -- tree-sitter-go spells a type-position `pkg.Name` as `qualified_type`,\nnot `selector_expression`, so every composite-literal constructor was being dropped and\nthe fixture reported 3 of 4).\n\nCorpus: caddy, prometheus, alertmanager, kserve, cosign, act, grype, dnscrypt-proxy,\ngocryptfs.\n\n  ASSIGNED candidate sites                                   143\n    blocked today (method name in ambiguous_names)           107\n    NO boundary reported anywhere today                       27\n      reachable by the ASSIGNMENT fix                          6   <- the answer\n      rooted at a COMPOSITE LITERAL, so it is NOT               21\n\nTHE DENOMINATOR DISTINCTION THAT DOES THE WORK. 116 of the 143 sites have a constructor\nthat is ITSELF a catalogued row (exec.Command, net.Dial, smtp.Dial ...), so the repo\nalready reports a boundary at that line. For those the method call is a second signal at\nthe sink, not a missing crossing. Quoting 143, or even 107, as \"the gap\" would oversell\nit by 24x / 18x. The residual value in that bucket is narrow but real: a taint that\nenters AFTER construction (cmd.Args = append(cmd.Args, userInput); cmd.Run()) is only\ncaught at the method call. Not measured, and not claimed.\n\nTHE DOMINANT ROW IS NOT AN ASSIGNMENT GAP AT ALL, which is why the split mattered.\nnet/http.Client.Do is 17 of the 27. With the positive control the earlier probe on this\nthread was missing (http.Get tags, so net/http is reachable):\n\n  (&http.Client{}).Do(r)          INLINE    -> 0 boundaries\n  c := &http.Client{}; c.Do(r)    ASSIGNED  -> 0 boundaries\n\nUnreachable in BOTH forms, because a composite literal offers no constructor CALL to\ntype from. That is a composite-literal receiver-typing gap, a different mechanism.\n(This supersedes the \"OPEN and unmeasured\" note on this thread about whether\nnet/http.Client.Do is reachable -- it is not, in any form, and now has a control.)\n\nThe asked-about gap is confirmed cleanly and separately on slog, where the constructor IS\na call:  l := slog.Default(); l.Info(m) -> 0   vs   slog.Default().Info(m) -> 1.\n\nWHAT TO BUILD INSTEAD, ranked by measured value rather than by novelty:\n\n  1. COMPOSITE-LITERAL receiver typing -- 21 sites, and the only security-relevant one.\n     `client := &http.Client{}; client.Do(req)` with a tainted URL is SSRF and is\n     invisible today in every syntactic form. net_send, not logging.\n  2. PARAM shape (typed parameter, `func h(c *gin.Context)`) -- 653 candidates, 145\n     blocked. Technically the easiest (Go parameters carry an explicit type, nothing to\n     infer) but the payoff is poor: ~136 of the 145 blocked are\n     slog.Logger.{Info,Error,Warn,Debug}, i.e. the `logging` boundary. The headline 653\n     is dominated by testing.T.TempDir, which ALREADY tags by short name.\n  3. The ASSIGNMENT gap itself -- 6 sites, 5 slog + 1 gin.Engine.Run. Same territory as\n     the python \"B\" mechanism that was measured at zero and recorded DO NOT BUILD.\n\nCAVEATS, load-bearing. The constructor->type map is HAND-BUILT from Go stdlib semantics,\nbecause the catalogue records which METHODS are I/O and not which functions RETURN the\nreceiving type; a missing row undercounts. Nine repos, so this is a corpus statement and\nnot a language one. And these are static-scan SITES, not production boundary CHAINS --\nthe python work established those are different populations (128 sites vs 125 chains),\nso do not compare this number against a chain count without re-deriving it."  # 3252
- op: discuss  # c5a8
  at: '2026-08-10T04:51:27Z'  # c5a8
  by: agent  # c5a8
  actor: jgstern_agent  # c5a8
  clock: 17  # c5a8
  nonce: c5a8  # c5a8
  message: "GO INSTANCE CLOSED, 2026-08-10: composite-literal receiver typing (L3, the receiver-\nevidence link).\n\nSHAPE. This class holds that a catalogued I/O call site must reach its catalogue entry\nregardless of receiver syntax. Go had a spelling that reached it in NO form:\n\n    var client http.Client      -> go:net/http:0-0:Do:unresolved   TYPED (worked)\n    client := http.Client{}     -> go:external:0-0:Do:unresolved   untyped\n    client := &http.Client{}    -> go:external:0-0:Do:unresolved   untyped\n\nTwo spellings of one declaration, two answers -- the class's own shape, and a DRIFTED\nCOPY rather than a missing feature. _type_identifier_from_node already handled\nqualified_type and its docstring says returning the full `http.Client` is \"critical for\nIO boundary detection\"; _type_from_rhs re-implemented a narrower type_identifier-only\nversion inline. Fix routes the copy through the existing predicate.\n\nROOT MECHANISM WORTH CARRYING TO OTHER LANGUAGES: tree-sitter spells a package-qualified\nname differently BY SYNTACTIC POSITION -- selector_expression in expression position,\nqualified_type in type position. Any handler that matches one silently drops the other.\nThis bit the sizing instrument too (caught by its positive control) and the fall-through\ncarried a `# pragma: no cover - defensive for unrecognized RHS` that ordinary\n&http.Client{} input reaches.\n\nMEASURED THROUGH L4 (the F3 gate), not at L2. This class's own warning is that emitting\nan edge buys nothing if it arrives untyped. 6 Go repos, both arms, call-edge count\nIDENTICAL (76,689): +25 boundaries tagged, net +76 intra-repo dsts, -746 `external`\nplaceholder slots. Positive control names the edge under test (PRE go:external Do tags 0,\nPOST go:net/http Do tags 1) after an earlier version of the control was found to pass in\nBOTH arms because http.NewRequest tags regardless.\n\nPRECISION HALF, which is the part that needed a second mechanism. Typing the receiver\nwalks it into the branch that strips `url.Values` to bare `Values` and looks that up in\nthe repo's OWN symbols -- reviving the alertmanager 13-spurious-in-edges bug the WI-jopar\nguard closed. New predicate _external_package_for_type: a type whose package is outside\nthis go.mod module cannot be defined by this repo, so the local lookup is skipped. On\ncaddy this REMOVES 48 false intra-repo edges, 17 of them pointing from production code at\na symbol defined in a _test.go file.\n\nNOTE FOR THE CLASS: finalize.py:382-386 already carries a downstream mitigation\n(demote_harmful_magnets) that REDIRECTS exactly two sub-classes -- \"a production->test-\nhelper misbind and a stdlib-interface-method shadow\". Those are the same edges this fix\nprevents at the producer. Worth checking, when the class is re-measured, how much of that\nmitigation's population is really an upstream receiver-typing gap in some language.\n\nNOT ADDRESSED, and separate: the constructor-CALL rooted assignment (`l := slog.New(...)`\nthen `l.Info(m)`) -- 6 of the 27 no-boundary sites. _type_from_constructor_call recognises\nonly the NewXxx naming convention, so `exec.Command` / `slog.Default` / `gin.Default`\nbind nothing. Sized at 6 sites and recorded as DON'T BUILD."  # c5a8
- op: discuss  # 413c
  at: '2026-08-10T06:22:46Z'  # 413c
  by: agent  # 413c
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 413c
  clock: 18  # 413c
  nonce: 413c  # 413c
  message: "MERGED as PR #260 (dev 21e15bcfa8). Tagged awaits_bakeoff_validation.\n\nWHAT IS ALREADY VALIDATED, and does not need a bakeoff: the edge-level and\nboundary-level deltas. They were measured directly by scripts/measure-go-composite-\nliteral-ab.py on 6 Go repos, both arms, whole diff, with a positive control naming the\nedge under test -- +25 boundaries tagged, call-edge count identical in both arms\n(76,689), -746 external placeholder slots, 48 false intra-repo edges removed on caddy.\nThat is a corpus measurement, not a projection.\n\nWHAT THE TAG IS ACTUALLY FOR -- the UNMEASURED downstream claim. Nothing here shows a\ntaint VERDICT moved. This class's own standing warning is that L2/L3 gains can measure\nas a win at the analyzer and produce zero findings (PR #231: 0 -> 154 DDG edges, zero\nfindings moved). The specific thing a bakeoff should answer:\n\n  1. Do any of the +25 boundaries become taint SINKS that participate in a flow?\n     net/http.Client.Do is a write-side network primitive, so it auto-derives a sink\n     (ADR-0017 section 2b) -- the SSRF shape is why this fix was chosen over the 6-site\n     assignment gap. Whether a real repo has a tainted URL reaching one is unknown.\n  2. Does removing 48 false intra-repo edges on caddy move centrality/dead-code\n     numbers? 17 of them pointed from production code at _test.go symbols, which is\n     exactly the shape that inflates in-degree on a test helper.\n  3. Direction check: no finding should DISAPPEAR. This change adds receiver evidence\n     and cannot itself turn a walk verdict to False, but it populates callees_at, and\n     since PR #214 a False earns `sanitized` and DROPS a flow. Unverified in the\n     conditions where it could matter -- the barrier arm has measured 0 walks on every\n     repo tried, so the suppressing path has never been observed running.\n\nSTRIP THE TAG only on evidence for 1 or 2; a null on both is a legitimate outcome and\nshould be recorded as such rather than as a failure."  # 413c
