  clock: 21  # 034c
  nonce: 034c  # 034c
  message: "PYTHON HALF MEASURED EXHAUSTIVELY AND PARTLY CLOSED, 2026-08-10 (dev 52acec99be).\nThis item's statement -- \"a catalogued I/O call site must reach its catalogue\nentry regardless of receiver syntax\" -- had only ever been measured INDIRECTLY\n(count sinks, count edges, count escapes). It is now measured DIRECTLY, for\nevery entry in the python catalogue at once.\n\nTHE INSTRUMENT (in-repo): scripts/measure-catalogue-reach.py generates one call\nsite per entry in load_catalog(lang).primitives, in BOTH import forms, and asks\nproduction for a boundary. Generating the fixture FROM the catalogue means the\ndenominator is the shipped catalogue and cannot drift from it.\n\n  over 215 expressible primitives (7 inexpressible, reported separately)\n                          BEFORE            AFTER\n    reachable BOTH ways   105  48.8%        153  71.2%\n    DOTTED form only       27  12.6%         16   7.4%\n    BARE form only          0   0.0%         41  19.1%\n    UNREACHABLE            83  38.6%          5   2.3%\n    unreachable by kind    function 5        function 5\n                           method  78        method   0\n\nROOT CAUSE, and it is the two-homes-for-one-fact shape. python.yaml declares a\nreceiver TYPE in the module slot of every kind:method primitive -- seventeen of\nthem. EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES, the only way the analyzer learns an external\nreceiver's type, was a hand-curated FOUR-row restatement naming three of those\nseventeen. Discriminating fixture through the real analyzer:\n\n    pathlib.Path(a).write_text(b)     -> calls python:pathlib.Path:...:write_text\n    smtplib.SMTP(a).sendmail(...)     -> NO method-call edge at all\n    sqlite3.Cursor(a).execute(b)      -> NO method-call edge at all\n\nFIXED by deriving the table from the catalogue, with a parity test that\nenumerates the LIVE catalogue so a type added to the YAML tomorrow fails the\ntest rather than silently going unreachable.\n\n*** REAL-CODE PAYOFF IS ZERO, AND THAT IS THE HEADLINE RATHER THAN THE 83->5. ***\nA/B at the analyzer (scripts/measure-ctor-table-derivation-ab.py, in-repo;\nmeasured there and NOT through io-boundaries because that path caches on repo\nstate + analyzer identity, which an in-process monkeypatch does not change, so\narm B would be served arm A's map):\n\n    hypergumbo   99,477 -> 99,477 call edges    gained 0   lost 0\n    pretix       62,272 -> 62,272               gained 0   lost 0\n\nWHY. Real code obtains most of these objects from FACTORY functions, not class\nconstructors: sqlite3.connect(path) then conn.cursor(), never sqlite3.Cursor(...).\nhypergumbo's own tracker cache is exactly that shape. The constructor table keys\non the TYPE NAME and can never fire for a factory-constructed type. Filed as\nWI-jidij with the split of which 15 types are constructor-shaped vs factory-shaped,\nand with the note that unlike the constructor table this CANNOT be catalogue-\nderived -- io_primitives records which METHODS are I/O, never which FUNCTIONS\nreturn the receiving type.\n\nA REGRESSION THIS CHANGE INTRODUCED, CAUGHT BY THE DIRECTION CHECK RATHER THAN BY\nREVIEW, and fixed before shipping. _process_call has a second consumer of the\ntable -- `elif callee_name in EXTERNAL_CONSTRUCTOR_TYPES` -- that emits\npython:builtins:0-0:<name>:unresolved, i.e. asserts the name IS a builtin, under a\ncomment claiming \"only the bare key `open` can match\". Seventeen new bare type\nnames falsified that immediately: pretix's `StreamWriter = codecs.getwriter('utf-8');\nStreamWriter(byte_data)` -- a LOCAL rebinding -- minted builtins.StreamWriter,\nwhile the sibling _external_constructor_type refused the same name a few lines\nearlier via its INV-kipor binding check. Now gated on BUILTIN_CONSTRUCTOR_NAMES,\nwhich already means exactly that; this also closes the pre-existing case where an\nunimported bare Path(...) minted builtins.Path.\n\nDIRECTION MEASURED BEFORE THE CHANGE: the 15 newly-mintable types carry 43 of 83\npython taint sources and 35 of 113 sinks, and 0 of 4 sanitizers -- so it cannot\narm the WI-fasub barrier arm where a False earns `sanitized`. That 0 was an\nUNCONTROLLED zero on first measurement: sanitizer rows carry only qualified_name,\nno kind, so a kind==\"method\" filter matched nothing and intersected everything to\nzero. Recovered by stripping the method off qualified_name; now pinned executably.\n\nSTATUS UNCHANGED (violated). The statement is not satisfied: 5 python primitives\nremain unreachable, 41 are reachable in one import form only (WI-lifol), the\nfactory shape is untouched (WI-jidij), and go/js/kotlin are unmeasured by this\ninstrument. What changed is that the python half now has an exhaustive number\ninstead of an anecdote, and a parity test that keeps it from silently regrowing."  # 034c
- op: discuss  # 4c10
  at: '2026-08-10T21:14:01Z'  # 4c10
  by: agent  # 4c10
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4c10
  clock: 22  # 4c10
  nonce: 4c10  # 4c10
  message: "WORDING CORRECTION to the 2026-08-10 note above, made rather than left standing.\n\nThat note called the probe's residual bucket \"UNREACHABLE\". It overstates. The\nverdict measures whether production named the CALLING FUNCTION in the chain's\nio_edge_src, so the accurate word is UNATTRIBUTED. All five residual primitives\n(subprocess.Popen, fileinput.FileInput, tempfile.{NamedTemporaryFile,\nTemporaryDirectory,TemporaryFile}) DO produce a chain -- anchored to\n`python:<external>:0-0:file:external_symbol`, a non-caller name. \"Sees the\nboundary, cannot say who called it\" is a different defect with a different owner\n(WI-pimun, which now carries a five-function reproducer) than \"cannot see the\nboundary\". The verdict table itself is unchanged: 105/27/0/83 before,\n153/16/41/5 after, reproduced back-to-back on separate workdirs.\n\nA SLOT-READING ERROR OF MY OWN, recorded because it nearly reached the owner as\na retraction. An attributed chain reads\n`python:probe.py:0-0:<probe_fn>:external_symbol` -- the caller sits in the NAME\nslot while the KIND slot reads `external_symbol` for EVERY chain on that fixture.\nI counted the kind slot, called it \"the anchor\", and concluded the instrument was\nnon-reproducible; two back-to-back runs then returned identical tables. The\ninstrument was fine and the reading was wrong. `entry_points` is empty on all 476\nchains there too, so it cannot discriminate either; the probe now keys on the\nNAME slot and prints both slots so the two cannot be confused again.\n\nNOTHING ELSE IN THE NOTE CHANGES. The analyzer-level evidence never went through\nio-boundaries: smtplib.SMTP(a).sendmail(...) and sqlite3.Cursor(a).execute(b)\nemitted NO method-call edge before the fix and a correctly typed one after, and\nthe real-repo A/B (0 gained / 0 lost on hypergumbo and pretix) was measured with\nanalyze_python directly. The \"real-code payoff is zero\" headline stands."  # 4c10
- op: discuss  # 5d88
  at: '2026-08-11T04:10:52Z'  # 5d88
  by: agent  # 5d88
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 5d88
  clock: 23  # 5d88
  nonce: 5d88  # 5d88
  message: "WI-lifol closed (PR #275): the dotted-module constructor path is now reachable. That leaves INV-linub's L4 catalogue-match path exercised for 12 more primitives across 4 types, verified end-to-end on envoy (+1 net_send chain, 0 lost).\n\nSCOPE CORRECTION worth carrying on this class item: the reach probe attributed 41 primitives to the dotted-constructor gap, but 29 of them are django.db.models, which is a MODULE and not a constructible type. A probe that generates one call site per catalogue entry will happily generate a form that cannot occur in real code -- 'models(a)' -- and score it as a miss. This is the fourth time on this class that a generated probe read as a payoff; the standing correction is to price the real-code floor before building, which measure-dotted-ctor-floor.py now does for this shape."  # 5d88
- op: discuss  # 945e
  at: '2026-08-11T17:51:45Z'  # 945e
  by: agent  # 945e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 945e
  clock: 24  # 945e
  nonce: 945e  # 945e
  message: "FIRST MULTI-LANGUAGE MEASUREMENT OF THIS CLASS. The generated reach probe now runs on\nGo, JavaScript and Java as well as Python -- previously it emitted Python source\nregardless of the language argument, resolved nothing, and its positive control correctly\nrefused to report. Full record: docs/measurements/0002-catalogue-reach-multilanguage.md.\n\n  language     attributed        residual\n  python       210/215 (97.7%)   5\n  javascript   182/187 (97.3%)   5\n  java         129/142 (90.8%)   13  (all db_read/db_write)\n  go           162/210 (77.1%)   48\n\nTHE GO RESULT IS THIS CLASS'S SHAPE, EXACTLY. Of Go's 48 unattributed entries, 45 are\nMETHOD-KIND and 3 are attributes -- i.e. 100% of Go's method-kind catalogue surface is\nunattributed while function-kind entries attribute at 100%. Concentrated in net_send (25),\nnet_recv (9), logging (5), subprocess (4). That is the same distribution Python showed\nbefore its receiver-typing work (78 of 83 misses method-kind), which makes Go the next\ntarget for a fix whose shape is already known rather than a new problem.\n\nThe 3 attribute misses (os.Stdin/Stdout/Stderr) DO produce a chain, anchored to a file\nnode rather than the calling function. Attribution defect, not a reach gap; counted\nseparately for that reason.\n\nCAVEAT THE NUMBERS CANNOT CARRY THEMSELVES: the form vocabularies differ per language\n(Go has ONE idiomatic form, Java's pair is instance/static), so 77.1% and 97.7% are not\nscored over the same thing and reading Go as \"worse than Python\" is a category error.\n\nAND THE FIXTURE SHAPE MOVED ONE ANSWER 35x. JavaScript with a function-local `require`\nattributes 4 of 187; the identical call with `const m = require('fs')` hoisted to module\nscope attributes 182. The final number uses module scope because that is the dominant\nidiom -- but a lazy `require` inside a function is ordinary JS and is 97.9% blind. That is\na live sub-case of this class's statement (\"regardless of receiver syntax\") that no filed\nitem currently covers."  # 945e
- op: discuss  # 8be7
  at: '2026-08-11T19:36:08Z'  # 8be7
  by: agent  # 8be7
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8be7
  clock: 25  # 8be7
  nonce: 8be7  # 8be7
  message: "GO ARM OF MEASUREMENT 0002 WITHDRAWN AND RE-RUN, 2026-08-11 (dev 1102e75bd3, PRs\nmerged). This item's Go number and the conclusion drawn from it were both wrong.\n\nWHAT WAS PUBLISHED UNDER THIS ITEM: go 162/210, \"100% of Go's method-kind\ncatalogue surface is unattributed\", and \"this is the same shape Python showed\nbefore its receiver-typing work, which makes Go the natural next target for the\nsame fix\". Both halves are retracted in docs/measurements/0002.\n\nTHE 45/45 MISS WAS THE PROBE'S RECEIVER SPELLING, not the catalogue. The fixture\nemitted every Go method call as a parenthesized composite literal. go.py types a\nreceiver only when the operand is a bare identifier, so that one spelling carries\nno receiver evidence. One file, one analyze_go run, only the spelling varying:\n\n  (&http.Client{}).Get(u)          -> go:external:0-0:Get:unresolved\n  var c http.Client; c.Get(u)      -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved\n  c := &http.Client{}; c.Get(u)    -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved\n  func(c *http.Client){ c.Get(u) } -> go:net/http:0-0:Get:unresolved\n\nTHE CONCLUSION WAS THE EXPENSIVE HALF. Go receiver typing already exists and\nworks; a session acting on that sentence would have built a mechanism the analyzer\nhas. This is the third time on this class that a confidently-written mechanism\nclaim has been retracted, and the pattern is the same each time: the instrument's\nshape was read as the subject's property.\n\nCORRECTED, with both spellings probed: 198/210 as the probe scores it, 178/210\n(84.8%) production-faithful. The 45 method entries split 16 genuinely reachable /\n20 unreachable-on-real-Go / 9 mis-credited.\n\nTHE CORRECTED NUMBER IS ALSO PART ARTEFACT, IN THE OPPOSITE DIRECTION, and the\ninstrument now discloses it rather than classifying it away: the probe's import\npath is the catalogue's own module slot, so for a third-party entry it writes\n`import (\"gin\")` — a line no Go program contains — and the entry scores reachable\nagainst a hint production can never emit. 20 method rows depend on that\nfabrication. Fixing only the receiver spelling would have RATIFIED them as\nreachable, which is why the spelling fix and the disclosure shipped together.\n\nTHIS ITEM'S STATEMENT SURVIVES INTACT and is still violated, but the residual\nunder it is now three named mechanisms rather than one unexamined bucket:\n  INV-javid  Go /vN semantic import versioning defeats alias derivation (P1,\n             recall; also the likely root cause of WI-tivij)\n  INV-safig  go.yaml third-party method rows use an unreachable module\n             vocabulary; ADR-0016 sec 27 says they should not ship (P2)\n  INV-vusum  kind-blind selection mis-credits 9 method calls to same-package\n             function rows; three candidate fixes already refuted (P2)\n  INV-tanom  Go call_construct is constant, so it cannot discriminate (P2)\n\nNone of the four is receiver typing. The catalogue-reach class is not, on Go, a\nreceiver-evidence problem.\n\nWHAT I DID NOT DO: re-check whether the Java \"instance-form zero\" recorded under\nthis item shares the fixture-shape mechanism. It superficially resembles it, and\nper the standing no-analogy rule that is a question, not a finding. java.py was\nnot read."  # 8be7
- op: discuss  # 8238
  at: '2026-08-11T22:34:40Z'  # 8238
  by: agent  # 8238
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8238
  clock: 26  # 8238
  nonce: '8238'  # 8238
  message: "GO'S CATALOGUE-REACH RESIDUAL IS NOW THREE NAMED MECHANISMS, TWO OF THEM CLOSED (dev 45280d90d3).\n\nMeasurement 0002's corrected Go arm split the 45 method entries 16 reachable / 20 unreachable-on-real-Go / 9 mis-credited. Status of each bucket:\n\n  20 unreachable  -> CLOSED by INV-safig (PR #295). They declared a package-identifier module (gin.Context) against an analyzer that emits the import path. Moved to docs/io-primitives-overlays/go-web-frameworks.yaml re-declared on the real path; they now produce 6 chains on an idiomatic fixture where they produced ZERO before, in either arm.\n  echo/fiber additionally needed INV-javid (PR #293) — a /vN import bound the alias 'vN', so they would have stayed unreachable however the catalogue spelled them. Both had to land for either to work.\n  9 mis-credited  -> STILL OPEN as INV-vusum. Not a reach gap: a chain exists under a same-package FUNCTION row. Three candidate fixes already refuted on that item — do not rediscover them.\n  16 reachable    -> nothing to do.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THIS CLASS'S STATEMENT. 'A catalogued I/O call site must reach its catalogue entry regardless of receiver syntax' is now, on Go, not primarily about receiver syntax at all. The measured residual is one selection defect (INV-vusum) plus whatever the 3 attribute-kind file-anchored entries turn out to be. Receiver typing — which this item's Go arm was believed to need for a month — was never the mechanism; it already worked.\n\nSTILL NOT CHECKED, and I am flagging it rather than assuming: whether Java's 'instance-form zero' recorded on this item shares the fixture-shape mechanism that explained Go's. It superficially resembles it. Per the no-analogy rule that is a question, not a finding; java.py has not been read."  # 8238
- op: discuss  # cb8a
  at: '2026-08-17T02:00:14Z'  # cb8a
  by: agent  # cb8a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cb8a
  clock: 27  # cb8a
  nonce: cb8a  # cb8a
  message: "FLEET CENSUS FOR THE F3 GATE, plus a boundary correction: INV-nizom is NOT an\ninstance of this class. 2026-08-16, dev 322a153915.\n\nTHE GATE DOES TWO JOBS AND THE OPEN ITEMS SPLIT ACROSS BOTH. gate_named_entry:\n\n    if call_construct == \"method\": return None          JOB 1, PRECISION:\n                                                        stop a METHOD call from\n                                                        matching a FUNCTION-kind\n                                                        entry (INV-tapat,\n                                                        INV-maluk).\n    non_method = [h for h in hits if h.kind != \"method\"] JOB 2, RECALL:\n                                                        drop METHOD-kind entries\n                                                        when there is no module\n                                                        hint. <- THIS CLASS\n\nThis item is job 2 -- a catalogued method-kind entry unreachable for want of\nreceiver evidence, a false NEGATIVE. INV-suril (java.nio.file.Files) is the same\njob. INV-nizom is job 1: a cpp METHOD matching a function-kind POSIX entry, a\nfalse POSITIVE. This item's own exposure table already implies the split and is\ncorrect as written -- \"c/cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell: 0% method-kind -- NOT at\nrisk from this class\" -- which is exactly why INV-nizom could not be an instance\nof it. INV-nizom's description asserts the three items are \"SAME CLASS\"; they\nare two directions through two branches of one function, and a single shared\nproducer-side rule would not have addressed both. Corrected on that item.\n\nFLEET CENSUS OF THE JOB-1 PRECONDITION (static, both spellings: the dict-literal\nform and the make_unresolved_edge kwarg form PR #227 introduced). Analyzers that\nstamp call_construct but can NEVER stamp \"method\", so job 1 is unreachable for\nthem entirely:\n\n    bash, clojure, commonlisp, elixir, elm, erlang, fsharp, groovy, haskell,\n    julia, kotlin, ocaml                                    -- 12\n    javascript/typescript: never stamps the field at all    -- INV-gijis\n\nRich stampers: go 10 sites, py 10, ruby 8, csharp 6, rust 3, dart 2, java 2,\nscala 2, swift 2, cpp 1 (now 2). KOTLIN IS THE CELL THIS CLASS SHOULD CARE\nABOUT: this item measures it at 95% method-kind sinks (89 of 93) with 0%\nemission, and it also cannot stamp \"method\", so it fails BOTH jobs.\n\nMEASURED, one full behavior map (whisper.cpp, 81,263 edges), rate = share of\nthat language's call edges carrying the field:\n\n    go          533 call edges   100.0%   method=448, function=85\n    bash        324             88.3%   function=286 only\n    ruby        186             87.6%   method=163\n    python      441             74.8%   method=330\n    cpp      34,983             41.1%   function=14,360, METHOD=1\n    kotlin      130             33.1%   function=43 only\n    java        304              9.9%   method=30\n    javascript  243              0.0%\n    c         7,829              0.0%   cuda 0.0%, glsl 0.0%\n\nTHE CENSUS TRAP, worth carrying: cpp reads as 41% covered and is 0.003% covered\nfor the gate, because the gate falls through on anything that is not literally\n\"method\" -- so \"function\" and absent are the SAME value to it. Any future\ncoverage metric for this class must count the METHOD share, not the field's\npresence. A presence-based census would have reported cpp, bash and kotlin as\npartially covered when job 1 is unreachable for all three.\n\nSECOND HOLE, MECHANISM READ, VOLUME NOT MEASURED -- stated as a hypothesis. The\nfleet emits 13 distinct call_construct values (function, method, constructor,\napplication, application_external, pipe, local, remote, remote_external,\nchained_return_type, interface_dispatch, macro_body, method_group) and job 1\ntests equality with \"method\" exactly. So go's interface_dispatch and\nchained_return_type and csharp's method_group -- all method-construct calls --\nfall through the refusal as if they were free functions. Whether that produces\nreal false positives is UNMEASURED; I did not size it.\n\nAND A WARNING FOR ANY FIX UNDER THIS CLASS, measured the hard way on INV-nizom.\ncall_construct == \"method\" does NOT mean \"instance method call\". Go spells a\npackage-qualified FUNCTION call as a selector expression, indistinguishable in\nshape from an instance call, and stamps both \"method\":\n\n    os.Open(p)   construct=method   module_slot=os          package FUNCTION\n    f.Close()    construct=method   module_slot=external    instance METHOD\n\nA consumer rule keyed on the construct alone removed 51 matches on whisper.cpp:\n2 intended and 49 TRUE positives (os.Open, os.Stat, fmt.Fprintln,\nnet/http.NewRequest, logging.exception, os.makedirs). The module hint is what\ndisambiguates those, which is why job 1 defers to it. This is the same\n\"capability is not recall\" trap this item already warns about, from the other\nsign -- and it lands on the SUPPRESSIVE side, so it deserves the same\ndirection-discipline paragraph."  # cb8a
- op: discuss  # ad3e
  at: '2026-08-17T02:06:27Z'  # ad3e
  by: agent  # ad3e
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ad3e
  clock: 28  # ad3e
  nonce: ad3e  # ad3e
  message: "NINE-LANGUAGE L2->L4 TABLE REFRESHED 2026-08-16 (dev 0d305fe0e4), using this\nitem's own instrument (meas/chain_probe.py, production _match_propagation_entry).\nThis is the artifact the item names as its closure condition. It is not closure --\ntwo cells are still VOID -- but the FRAME HAS MOVED and that is the headline.\n\n    lang        CONTROL               TWOSTEP               CHAINED\n    go          emit+match            emit+match            emit,NOMATCH(method)\n    java        emit,NOMATCH          emit+match            emit,NOMATCH\n    javascript  emit+match            NO EDGE               NO EDGE\n    kotlin      emit+match            NO EDGE               NO EDGE\n    objc        NO EDGE               emit,NOMATCH          emit,NOMATCH\n    python      emit+match            emit+match            emit+match\n    rust        emit,NOMATCH          emit,NOMATCH          emit,NOMATCH\n    scala       emit,NOMATCH(method)  emit,NOMATCH(method)  emit,NOMATCH\n    swift       emit+match            emit,NOMATCH(method)  emit,NOMATCH(method)\n\n1. THE L2 STORY IS LARGELY OVER; L3 IS THE BINDING CONSTRAINT. Seven of nine\n   languages now EMIT on both method-call forms. Only kotlin and javascript\n   still produce NO EDGE (WI-nasuf's cell, unchanged). This is precisely what\n   this item predicted -- \"making an analyzer emit an edge for conn.Write(buf)\n   buys NOTHING if the edge arrives with no receiver type\" -- and it is now the\n   measured majority case rather than a forecast. The per-link detail shows\n   where: every NOMATCH row carries module_hint=external.\n\n2. PYTHON IS CLEAN ON ALL THREE SITES -- the first language to be. Published\n   was (True, False); it is now (True, True) on emission AND matching, via\n   module_hint=pathlib.Path on both forms. Constructor receiver typing did it.\n   Isolated by shape on a purpose-built fixture:\n\n       p = Path(raw); p.write_text(x)     module slot pathlib.Path   MATCHES\n       Path(raw).write_text(x)            module slot pathlib.Path   MATCHES\n       obj.related.create()               module slot external       no match\n       obj.store.path.write_text(raw)     module slot external       no match\n\n   So python's remaining gap is the ATTRIBUTE chain, not the CONSTRUCTOR chain,\n   and it is an L3 gap. Recorded separately on INV-mumov and WI-makij, whose\n   headlines this refutes.\n\n3. GO'S CELL IS FILLED -- it was one of the three this item listed as unmeasured.\n   Control passes, so the datum is admissible. go TWOSTEP now matches through\n   module_hint=net/http with is_resolved=False, i.e. LEGITIMATELY through the\n   module filter -- NOT the ungated bare-name hits[0] path INV-fazim filed\n   (which had module_hint=external and is_resolved=True). Composite-literal\n   receiver typing appears to have closed it. INV-fazim should be re-checked\n   against this before more work goes into it.\n\n4. RUST AND OBJC REMAIN VOID, and now with a diagnosis rather than a shrug.\n   Their CONTROL sites fail: objc emits NO EDGE for a module-qualified free\n   function, rust emits but does not match. A broken control means the two nulls\n   beside it are not evidence (rule 3) -- but the control failing is itself a\n   finding about those languages, and it is the thing to fix before their cells\n   can be filled. java and scala controls are also broken.\n\n5. THE INSTRUMENT'S REGRESSION GUARD FIRES ON STALE-BASELINE DRIFT, and would\n   have voided the new cells for the wrong reason. It compares against WI-nasuf's\n   published cells and declares \"1 control language(s) disagree ON EMISSION ...\n   Treat the go/rust/objc cells as VOID\". The disagreeing language is PYTHON, and\n   the disagreement is item 2 above -- a real improvement that landed after the\n   baseline was published on 2026-08-05. A guard that cannot distinguish \"the\n   probe is wrong\" from \"the code got better\" will void every future run of a\n   subsystem under active repair. The guard should compare against a baseline\n   carrying the dev SHA it was measured at, and report drift as drift."  # ad3e
