  nonce: '0465'  # 0465
  message: "RUST'S L2 CELL IS NOW MEASURED. This item's table records\n\"go, rust, objc  NOT MEASURED  <- this class closes it\". Rust is filled here.\n\nMEASURED 2026-08-26 on merged dev bd638f8cbf, live tool, not a fixture:\nencrypted-dns-server (langs rust+toml only, so nothing else can be producing the\nsignal) returns `inconclusive` on all 7 generic claims, and the withholding\nreason is THIS CLASS verbatim:\n\n  \"the analysis calls into 2 module(s) whose catalogued I/O is method-shaped\n   (std::fs::File, std::path::Path) but produced no method call edge for any of\n   them, so their I/O is structurally invisible.\"\n\nThat is `method_starved_modules` in verify_claims.py:3219 -- the L2 failure this\nclass describes, in production, for rust. Rust sits at 53% method-kind\n(16 of 30 sinks) per this item's own exposure table, so the cell is not a\ncuriosity: it gates over half the rust sink catalogue.\n\nWHAT IS *NOT* ESTABLISHED, so the cell is filled honestly rather than\ngenerously: this shows the two catalogued method-shaped modules the repo reaches\nproduce no method call edge. It does NOT show that EVERY rust method call fails\nto emit, and I did not read the declining branch in the rust analyzer. This\nitem's own closure criterion asks for the branch READ, not inferred. So the cell\nmoves from NOT MEASURED to MEASURED-IN-PRODUCTION-ON-ONE-REPO, and the fixture\nmeasurement WI-nasuf did for kotlin/javascript is still owed for rust.\n\nAND THIS ITEM'S SEQUENCING RULE NOW HAS A SECOND WORKED INSTANCE. The rule as\nfiled: \"Completeness and reachability MULTIPLY: adding method-kind entries to\nkotlin's catalogue returns exactly zero while 0% of kotlin method calls emit an\nedge. So L2/L3 is sequenced first and L1 follows.\"\n\nWI-lutuh IS AN L1 ITEM, and its recommended order starts with rust. Measured\nagainst the ladder in `boundary_coverage`, that order is inverted for rust --\nsee the note I am filing on WI-lutuh. Kotlin was the argument; rust is now the\nsecond instance, and it was found by trying to do the L1 work."  # 0465
- op: discuss  # 8653
  at: '2026-08-26T16:35:45Z'  # 8653
  by: agent  # 8653
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8653
  clock: 36  # 8653
  nonce: '8653'  # 8653
  message: "CORRECTION TO MY OWN NOTE FILED EARLIER TODAY. I wrote that rust's\nmethod_starved_modules result is \"the L2 failure this class describes\". IT IS\nNOT L2. It is L3, and this item's own four-link chain is what makes the\ndifference actionable rather than cosmetic.\n\nMEASURED on encrypted-dns-server's own cached survey (langs rust+toml only),\ncounting rust-dst edges directly rather than inferring from the gate's message:\n\n  rust dst edges                     1183\n  call_construct = method             219      <- L2 PASSES\n  call_construct = function           196\n  call_construct = <none>             745\n  call_construct = macro_body          23\n\n  module slot on the 219 METHOD-construct edges:\n      external            203   (92.7%)\n      src/cache.rs          5\n      src/crypto.rs         4\n      src/dnscrypt_certs.rs 4\n      src/config.rs         2\n      src/rate_limiter.rs   1\n      std::fs::File         0\n      std::path::Path       0\n\nSO THE CHAIN BREAKS AT L3, NOT L2. The rust analyzer DOES emit a method-construct\nedge -- 219 of them -- and it DOES populate call_construct, so it is not in the\nJS/TS abstain class either. What it cannot do is TYPE THE RECEIVER: 92.7% of its\nmethod edges carry the `external` placeholder in the module slot, and not one\ncarries a stdlib module. L4 then refuses them correctly, exactly as this item\nsays it must (an untyped method call must not match a method-kind entry --\nstr.replace must not match Path.replace).\n\nRUST IS THEREFORE A LIVE INSTANCE OF THIS ITEM'S CENTRAL WARNING, which was\nargued from kotlin and is now demonstrated on a second language: \"making an\nanalyzer emit an edge for conn.Write(buf) buys NOTHING if the edge then arrives\nwith no receiver type, because L4 correctly refuses it.\" For rust the emission\nfix is ALREADY DONE and bought exactly what the warning predicts -- 219 edges,\nzero matches.\n\nTHE SHAPE IS INV-mumov's, IN A DIFFERENT LANGUAGE. INV-mumov is filed as \"Python\nattribute-chain receivers carry no module hint (module slot 'external'), so\ncatalogued method-kind sinks reached that way never match\". Replace Python with\nRust and the sentence holds unedited at 92.7%. I am NOT folding rust into\nINV-mumov -- they are per-analyzer, which is this class's whole premise -- but\nthe class now has two languages failing at the SAME link for what may be the\nsame reason, which is the chokepoint question this item exists to answer.\n\nWHAT IS STILL NOT DONE, stated so the cell is not over-claimed: I have not read\nWHY rust's receiver typing declines on std types. rust.py carries real typing\nmachinery (file-scoped var_types, a method_return_type_registry, and a\nreceiver-typed emission path stamping receiver=\"typed_field\"), so this is not an\nanalyzer that never tries -- it is one whose typing does not reach std. That\nbranch read is still owed, and it is the difference between \"add std return\ntypes to the registry\" and something structural.\n\nTABLE CELL, corrected:\n    rust    L2 PASSES (219 method edges, call_construct populated)\n            L3 FAILS  (92.7% module slot `external`, 0 stdlib)\n            measured in production on one repo; fixture measurement still owed"  # 8653
- op: discuss  # 9e0a
  at: '2026-08-27T04:51:43Z'  # 9e0a
  by: agent  # 9e0a
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9e0a
  clock: 37  # 9e0a
  nonce: 9e0a  # 9e0a
  message: "RUST ROW FOR THIS ITEM'S EXPOSURE TABLE, MEASURED AT FINDINGS LEVEL (WI-gihos,\n2026-08-27). The table in this item's description reports per-language SINK\nCOUNTS and the method-kind share of them (kotlin 93/89, java 69/67, ...). That\nis an L1 census -- how much of the catalogue is method-keyed. This is the L4\nconsequence for rust, measured on the thing the item says a fix must be\nmeasured through: FINDINGS, not edges.\n\nINSTRUMENT: production loader + production classify_call_in_catalog, over the\n42 reusable surveys in lutuh_scope_08242026. 11,691 rust CALL edges examined.\n\nRESULT: the rust catalogue matches ZERO method-kind primitives. Not \"few\" --\nzero. Every match it makes across the whole cohort is function-kind:\nstd::env.var 11, std::io.stdin 10, std::fs.create_dir_all 9, std::env.var_os 8,\nstd::io.stdout 5, std::fs.copy 3, std::fs::File.open 2, ... 23 distinct\nfunction-kind rows fire; 0 of ~30 method-kind rows do.\n\nTHE EDGES ARE THERE AND THEY ARE REAL. 78 genuine trait-method call sites in\nthe cohort: write_all 46, read_to_end 15+1, read_to_string 9+1+2, read_line 6.\nEvery one of them arrives as\n\n    rust:external:0-0:<name>:external_symbol   with dst_ref = null\n\nso module_hint is `external`, gate_named_entry drops the method-kind hit\n(call_construct == \"method\" returns None unconditionally), and the short-name\nroute is closed too because read/read_to_end/read_to_string/write/write_all/\nflush are all in rust.yaml ambiguous_names. BOTH ROUTES SHUT. Only 205 of the\n11,691 rust call edges carry a dst_ref key at all, and the sampled ones are\nnull -- so this is not an artifact of my instrument passing dst_ref=None; there\nis no better hint on the wire to pass.\n\nWHY THIS IS WORTH RECORDING HERE rather than as a new item: it is exactly this\nitem's L3/L4, already named in root_cause, and it makes the consequence\nconcrete for one language -- EVERY method row in rust.yaml is dead today.\nstd::net::TcpStream's read/write_all, std::process::Command's spawn/output/\nstatus, the 11 std::fs::File methods WI-bupor shipped, and the 40 WI-gihos just\nshipped. A file read and a socket read are equally invisible in rust.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR ANY FIX SCOPED HERE: the rust arm has a clean before/after\nnumber available. Today it is 0 method-kind matches / 78 available trait-method\ncall sites. That is a better tripwire than an edge count, and it is the shape\nthis item asks for (\"measured through L4, on findings, not through L2 on\nedges\")."  # 9e0a
- op: discuss  # 4ab3
  at: '2026-08-28T02:28:15Z'  # 4ab3
  by: agent  # 4ab3
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4ab3
  clock: 38  # 4ab3
  nonce: 4ab3  # 4ab3
  message: "THE EXPOSURE TABLE PREDICTS FLOW PRODUCTION. INDEPENDENT COHORT, OUTCOME-LEVEL,\nMEASURED THROUGH L4 ON FINDINGS -- the axis this item demands.\n\nINSTRUMENT: the 54-repo screening in frame_08252026/screen (run 2026-08-25,\ngeneric-taint-claims.yaml, 1025 flows over 37 producing repos). Flows attributed\nby the flow's OWN source-symbol language, not the cohort directory (LIVE.md\nr12). \"repos present\" comes from each run's dataflow_coverage.languages census,\nso a zero is distinguished from an absent language (r8).\n\n    language     method-kind%   repos   flows   flows/repo\n    java              97%          3       0        0.0\n    scala             97%          1       0        0.0\n    kotlin            95%          3       0        0.0\n    objc              90%          2       0        0.0\n    swift             87%          4       0        0.0\n    rust              53%         11       2        0.2\n    python            46%         15      50        3.3\n    javascript        34%         28      31        1.1\n    go                27%         16     303       18.9\n    c                  0%          9      29        3.2\n    cpp                0%          1       0        0.0\n    elixir             0%          2      26       13.0\n    erlang             0%          2     306      153.0\n    haskell            0%          2      81       40.5\n\nThe five languages this item's table puts at >=87% method-kind are present in 13\nrepos and produce ZERO flows between them. Every language at <=53% produces\nflows -- 828 across 86 repo-appearances -- with one exception, cpp, which had a\nsingle repo in the cohort and is the one cell where \"the cohort lacks the repo\"\nis still the honest reading.\n\nWHY THIS IS CONFIRMATION AND NOT RESTATEMENT: the table was written 2026-08-06\nfrom the catalogue alone, as a claim about EXPOSURE. The screening is a\ndifferent cohort, a different date, and measures OUTCOME. It was not built to\ntest this item. The ordering is monotone across ten languages.\n\nRULES OUT THE COMPETING EXPLANATIONS, each checked rather than argued:\n  - Not catalogue absence. The production loader gives every one of these\n    languages both halves: java 57/69 src/sink, kotlin 54/93, scala 70/76,\n    swift 25/56, objc 32/62, rust 63/84. INV-potuf's typescript defect (sources\n    but no sinks) does NOT recur anywhere -- and typescript, once fixed, now\n    produces 29 flows.\n  - Not the DDG blockers. The three blockers the census reports for these\n    languages (cfg_mapping, atomic_statement, def_use_extractor) are also\n    reported for bash, which produces 168 flows structurally. go and rust carry\n    NO blockers and sit at 303 and 2.\n  - Not cohort absence, except for cpp (n=1).\n\nTHE CLEANEST CONTROL IN THE SET IS RUST vs GO. Both carry zero blockers, both\nare compiled and statically typed, both are well represented (11 and 16 repos).\nGo produces 303 flows and rust produces 2. That isolates the defect to receiver\nevidence rather than to method, language family, or cohort -- which is what this\nitem's L3/L4 already says, now with an outcome number attached.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE BASELINE (INV-duvup): a series described as measuring \"the\ntool\" today measures erlang/go/bash/haskell/python/js/c/ts/elixir. Five\nlanguages contribute structurally nothing, and it is this item that decides\nwhether they ever do."  # 4ab3
