  by: agent  # d929
  actor: jgstern_agent  # d929
  clock: 51  # d929
  nonce: d929  # d929
  message: "Tag stripped in the 2026-09-04 queue audit (phase 2, E-misapplied) -- STATUS UNCHANGED, this item stays violated and P1. Rationale: awaits_bakeoff_validation names a single quantitative improvement claim that a cohort can move. This is a CLASS item; it carries no such number of its own, and each per-language instance ships with its own A/B measured through L4 and is validated in its own PR (java INV-vugon/WI-gajuh, kotlin WI-nasuf, python INV-mumov, swift INV-kotob/WI-higob, rust INV-pamis, js INV-misup). Leaving the tag on the rollup makes the queue report a validation that no cohort run can ever discharge, which is how the queue accumulates the anomalies the drain playbook exists to clear. The tag belongs on the instances; INV-mumov still carries it. Audit: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/bakeoff_queue_09042026/PHASE1_CLASSIFICATION.md"  # d929
- op: discuss  # 4792
  at: '2026-09-04T17:55:39Z'  # 4792
  by: agent  # 4792
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 4792
  clock: 52  # 4792
  nonce: '4792'  # 4792
  message: "FOUR MORE L3 CELLS MOVED, AND A RESULT THAT CHANGES HOW THIS CLASS MUST BE MEASURED. 2026-09-04, dev ef9c55f8bf. Reporting only -- no status change, no re-prioritisation; the class stays violated and P1.\n\nL3 MOVEMENT SHIPPED SINCE THE JAVA ENTRY (2026-09-03), each PR A/B'd with every moved row read back at source:\n  swift  #765  chained receiver typing (INV-kotob / WI-higob family): a call on the result of a typed call carries the owner path.\n  swift  #768/#772  argument-label overload binding and the grammar-lag reparse, which decide WHICH overload a site binds and therefore which owner path the slot gets.\n  objc   #769  loop and binding receivers: for-in and declaration binders type the ELEMENT. A first cut typed the ITERATED COLLECTION instead ([paths count] NSArray -> NSString) and was caught only by reading the re-key column -- losses read 0 throughout.\n  go     #769  a package-qualified producer consults the return-type registry.\n  rust   #770  Result/Option projector and the ? operator unwrapping on the written path (WI-papar).\nPer-language cells now: java, swift, objc, go, rust have a declared/derivable-receiver path at L3; python has two slices (#752 construct-and-assign, #756 project-class field maps) with the factory-function and non-Name-root producers open; kotlin and javascript remain unswept at L2 (WI-nasuf).\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT RESULT, which is the part to carry. Across those four consecutive receiver-typing changes, io-boundaries moved by ZERO on every repository measured: Alamofire 40/30, Vernissage 8/8, AFNetworking 22/22, SDWebImage 34/30, caddy 299, gocryptfs 253, guacamole 93, ripgrep 30/25. Not one chain gained or lost.\n\nWHY, read in the code rather than inferred. gate_named_entry is the SHARED no-module-hint decision for all three consumers, and lookup_with_module reaches it only after falling through to the short-name path (allow_short_name_fallback, default True). The gate refuses a METHOD-kind row without a hint -- which is the L4 refusal this item's description already names, and it is intact. What the zero shows is that the reach io-boundaries had on these repos was already coming through the short-name / function-kind path, traced on a minimal go fixture where the boundary was reached with an empty slot.\n\nWHAT THIS DOES TO THIS ITEM'S OWN THESIS. The description says an L2-only fix buys nothing because L4 refuses. That remains true. The new fact is the mirror of it and was not anticipated here: an L3 fix can also read as zero at the io-boundaries consumer, for the OPPOSITE reason -- that consumer did not need the slot to reach those rows. So filling the slot buys PRECISION (the row reached is now the right one, verified against the receiver type) rather than RECALL, on this corpus.\n\nTHE MEASUREMENT RULE THAT FOLLOWS, and the reason this is filed on the class rather than on an instance: an L3 fix under this class must be measured through the TAINT path (_lookup_named_entry, on findings), not through io-boundaries chain counts. Measured the latter way it will read as inert and be judged wrongly. This item's existing rule -- \"measured through L4, on findings, not through L2 on edges\" -- is correct and was followed; what it did not say is that io-boundaries chain count is not that metric.\n\nWHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, said plainly. The corpus-wide zero does not separate \"the boundary was already reached by short name\" from \"the newly typed sites are not catalogued sinks at all\". The go fixture demonstrates the first mechanism exists; it does not show it accounts for all eight repositories. The discriminating experiment is a repository where the short-name gate REFUSES -- a catalogued METHOD-kind primitive whose short name is in the language's ambiguous_names set, reached through an untyped receiver -- and none of the eight was selected for that property. Until that repository exists, no payoff figure for a receiver-typing item under this class should be quoted in io findings.\n\nCORRECTION TO THIS ITEM'S OWN TEXT: the description's L2 status table still reads \"python two-step emits; chained NO EDGE\". That has been false since 2026-08-17 and confirmed false at tip today on the filed fixture (INV-mumov's 2026-09-04 entry). The table is left in place as the 2026-08-05 measurement it is labelled as; this entry is the correction of record."  # 4792
- op: discuss  # 1c14
  at: '2026-09-04T17:56:50Z'  # 1c14
  by: agent  # 1c14
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 1c14
  clock: 53  # 1c14
  nonce: 1c14  # 1c14
  message: "CORRECTION TO THE ENTRY ABOVE, same day, before it travels: two of its PR attributions are wrong. The measurement result and the conclusion drawn from it are unaffected -- what was wrong is which number goes with which PR, which is exactly the kind of error that makes a later reader re-run the wrong arm.\n\nVERIFIED MAPPING, taken from the auto-pr run logs (branch name and merged commit per PR), not from memory:\n\n  #765  jgstern-agent/fix/swift-chained-receiver-typing        swift: a receiver that is an EXPRESSION carries its type (WI-higob slice 2)\n  #767  jgstern-agent/fix/swift-grammar-lag-rewrite            swift: reparse an unparseable file through a byte-preserving rewrite (INV-bisok)\n  #768  jgstern-agent/fix/swift-argument-label-binding         swift: a call binds a project member only when some overload's ARGUMENT LABELS admit it (INV-fatap)\n  #769  jgstern-agent/feat/library-signature-catalogue         the library-signature catalogue, incl. the go package-qualified producer consulting the return-type registry (WI-lalot)\n  #770  jgstern-agent/fix/rust-chained-receiver-module-slot    rust: chained receiver, the ? and unwrap forms (WI-papar)\n  #772  jgstern-agent/fix/loop-and-binding-receivers           swift AND objc: a receiver bound by a LOOP or an optional-binding carries its type (WI-higob residual)\n  #774  jgstern-agent/fix/sink-before-source-verdict-neutrality  taint verdict neutrality (INV-muhij) -- not a receiver-typing change\n\nWHAT THE ENTRY ABOVE GOT WRONG:\n  1. It attributed the objc loop/binding receivers to #769. They are in #772, and that PR is swift AND objc, not objc alone.\n  2. It attributed the swift grammar-lag reparse to \"#768/#772\". The reparse is #767; #768 is argument-label binding alone and #772 is the loop/binding work.\nCorrect in the entry above: swift chained #765, rust #770, and the go registry consumer riding in #769.\n\nTHE ZERO-io RESULT IS UNCHANGED and rests on the same repositories (Alamofire 40/30, Vernissage 8/8, AFNetworking 22/22, SDWebImage 34/30, caddy 299, gocryptfs 253, guacamole 93, ripgrep 30/25), each arm cold, with every moved per-site row read back at source. So is the conclusion: the reach io-boundaries had on these repositories was already coming through the short-name / function-kind path, filling the slot bought precision rather than recall on this corpus, and an L3 fix under this class must be measured through the taint path rather than through io-boundaries chain counts.\n\nONE THING THE ENTRY ABOVE STATED MORE CONFIDENTLY THAN THE RECORD SUPPORTS, corrected here rather than left: it presented the io-boundaries zero as spanning \"four consecutive receiver-typing changes\" and then listed five PRs. The per-PR pairing of repository to arm is recorded in each item's own thread and lab directory (higob2_chained_, higob3_loops_, fatap_labels_, lalot_libsigs_, papar_rust_chained_); what is established at the class level is the aggregate -- no repository measured showed an io-boundaries chain move across any of these receiver-typing changes -- not a per-PR attribution of each repository's number."  # 1c14
