- op: discuss  # 8645
  at: '2026-09-09T16:29:34Z'  # 8645
  by: agent  # 8645
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8645
  clock: 75  # 8645
  nonce: '8645'  # 8645
  message: "PHASE 1 COMPLETE 2026-09-09 -- THE EXPOSURE TABLE IN THIS ITEM'S OWN DESCRIPTION\nIS FALSIFIED. Owner-authorised. Full record + instruments:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/linub_enumeration_09092026/ (PLAN.md pre-registered\nbefore any number existed, census.py, runall.sh, analyze.py, RESULT.md).\n\nTHE FALSIFIED LINE, verbatim from the description:\n    \"c/cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell: 0% method-kind -- NOT at risk from this class.\"\nIt is false for all five. Every one carries rows the catalogue ITSELF declares\nambiguous, which the F3 gate refuses without module context -- the route\nWI-lajus filed for C, which this class's frame never modelled.\n\nCONTROLS, both pre-registered, both held: C reproduces the WI-lajus shape\n(R1=0, R2>0); kotlin reproduces the table's 95% method-kind (181/186). The\nnegative control (scoring a repo against a FOREIGN catalogue) collapses as\nrequired: phoenix-framework reaches 53 on elixir's catalogue, 4 on swift's,\n0 on kotlin's.\n\nSTRUCTURAL EXPOSURE (census over all 15 shipped catalogues, no sample; R1 =\nmethod-kind rows, R2 = rows whose name is in the catalogue's own\nambiguous_names). Dismissed languages:\n    c       115 rows   R1   0 (0%)   R2  16 (14%)\n    cpp     121 rows   R1   0 (0%)   R2  16 (13%)\n    elixir  529 rows   R1   0 (0%)   R2 133 (25%)\n    erlang  278 rows   R1   0 (0%)   R2  59 (21%)\n    haskell 276 rows   R1   0 (0%)   R2  10 (4%)\nNOT comparable to the old table's percentages: its denominator was SINKS ONLY\n(113 for python), this is ALL ROWS (659). The finding -- R1=0 does not imply\nnot-at-risk -- is denominator-independent.\n\nC IS A CONFIRMED FAIL AT L4, WITH ITS CONTROL INSIDE THE SAME REPO. On qemu-dtc\n(a SECOND C repository, independent of WI-lajus's socket corpus), same analyzer,\nsame run:\n    fopen  not ambiguous  7 sites  REACHES stdio.fopen\n    fread  not ambiguous  6 sites  REACHES stdio.fread\n    fwrite not ambiguous  2 sites  REACHES stdio.fwrite\n    open   AMBIGUOUS      6 sites  reaches nothing\n    write  AMBIGUOUS      6 sites  reaches nothing\n    close  AMBIGUOUS      5 sites  reaches nothing\n    read   AMBIGUOUS      1 site   reaches nothing\nThe ONLY property separating fopen from open is ambiguous-name membership.\nPositive and negative control in one file, one run.\n\nTHE OTHER FOUR ARE \"NOT BLIND\", WHICH IS WEAKER THAN A PASS. elixir reaches\n12/134 R2 rows on phoenix-framework, erlang 14/60 on rebar3, haskell 1/10 on\nservant, cpp 4/22 on modsecurity -- so module context does arrive at the call\nsite at least sometimes (elixir `File.read` and erlang `file:read_file` are\nwritten module-qualified). NOT-REACHED IS NOT A FAILURE COUNT: a row can be\nunreached because the repo never calls it. This classifies cells; it does not\nscore them.\n\nTHE UNEXAMINED SET -- phase 1's actual output:\n  * objc IS THE LARGEST HOLE AND IT IS NOT THE C ROUTE. 123 of 131 rows are\n    method-kind (94%), the third-highest exposure of any language, and there is\n    NO filed item and NO corpus measurement for it anywhere. It sat in the old\n    table the whole time and nobody went back to it.\n  * java / scala / swift rest on WI-nasuf's FIXTURE measurement (2026-08-05),\n    not corpus. java's R1uR2 is 100% of rows, scala's 90%. Fixture evidence at\n    that exposure is thin.\n  * cpp, elixir, erlang have NO filed item at all.\n\nDISCLOSED INSTRUMENT DEFECT: census.py dedups by qualified_name (C R2 = 16),\nanalyze.py counts raw rows (C R2 = 22). Both are legitimate counts of different\nthings, but they are two homes for one fact; the gap is duplicate\nqualified_names in the catalogue. Neither number changes any verdict above.\n\nTHE DECISION THIS FORCES, for the owner:\n  A. WIDEN. File the six cells, drain them, close on a class-level\n     re-measurement. ~6 new items on a P1 open since August.\n  B. RE-SCOPE. Narrow this statement to the RECEIVER-SYNTAX mechanism it was\n     actually framed around, and file the ambiguous-name / no-module-slot route\n     as its OWN class with WI-lajus as first instance and cpp/elixir/erlang/\n     haskell/c as its cells.\n\nRECOMMENDING B. The two mechanisms have different fixes (receiver typing vs\nmodule-context recovery), different language sets, and different apexes -- and a\nclass whose own exposure table cannot predict its counterexamples is measuring\nsomething other than what it names. Under B this item closes on the languages it\nactually models; objc and the java/scala/swift fixture-evidence gap stay with\nit, because those ARE receiver-syntax cells."  # 8645
- op: update  # ce65
  at: '2026-09-09T16:55:07Z'  # ce65
  by: agent  # ce65
  actor: jgstern_agent  # ce65
  clock: 76  # ce65
  nonce: ce65  # ce65
  set:  # ce65
    description: "CLASS ITEM. Filed 2026-08-06 under LIVE.md rule 7 (\"third per-site item of a\nkind => file the CLASS\"). Its instances are WI-makij, INV-mumov and WI-nasuf,\nwhich are three shapes of ONE defect: a call site of a catalogued I/O primitive\ndoes not reach its catalogue entry because of the RECEIVER SYNTAX used to call\nit. None of them is a duplicate of the others; all three are per-analyzer, and\nthe class exists to hold the shared invariant, the shared measurement, and the\nchokepoint question none of them can answer alone.\n\nWHY THE INVARIANT IS ABOUT MATCHING AND NOT ABOUT EMISSION. Read in\ntaint.py::_match_propagation_entry / _lookup_named_entry, 2026-08-06. Reaching\na catalogue entry is a CHAIN, and the three filed items sit at only the first\nlink:\n\n  L1  the entry exists in io_primitives/<lang>.yaml         (completeness)\n  L2  the analyzer emits a call edge for the call site      (emission)\n  L3  the edge carries receiver evidence -- a module_hint,\n      or a qualified callee name                            (evidence)\n  L4  _lookup_named_entry accepts it                        (the F3 gate)\n\nL4 is a DELIBERATE refusal, not a bug: an untyped method call\n(call_construct == \"method\") with no usable module hint never matches a\nmethod-kind entry (io-boundary:F3, INV-tapat/INV-maluk -- str.replace must not\nmatch Path.replace), and a name in the language's ambiguous_names set is\nrefused for the same reason. go.yaml alone lists 28 ambiguous names including\nRead, Write, Close, Get and Do.\n\nCONSEQUENCE, and the reason this class is filed before any fix is written:\n**making an analyzer emit an edge for `conn.Write(buf)` buys NOTHING if the\nedge then arrives with no receiver type, because L4 correctly refuses it.**\nA fix scoped to L2 alone would be measured as a win at the analyzer and produce\nzero new findings -- the exact capability-is-not-recall failure WI-nonad's A/B\nalready recorded once (0 -> 154 DDG edges, 0 findings moved). Any fix under\nthis class must be measured through L4, on findings, not through L2 on edges.\n\nMEASURED EXPOSURE, TWO-DIMENSIONAL (re-measured 2026-09-09, phase 1;\ninstruments and pre-registration in\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/linub_enumeration_09092026/).\n\nTHE 2026-08-06 VERSION OF THIS TABLE WAS ONE-DIMENSIONAL AND IT WAS FALSIFIED.\nIt ranked languages by method-kind share alone and concluded \"c/cpp/elixir/\nerlang/haskell: 0% method-kind -- NOT at risk from this class.\" That line is\nFALSE FOR ALL FIVE and has been excised rather than annotated. Every one of them\ncarries rows the catalogue ITSELF declares ambiguous, which L4 refuses without\nmodule context. A table that cannot predict this class's own counterexamples is\nnot a frame, and this is the second route the one-dimensional frame missed (the\nfirst was go -- see INV-tanom, where the module_hint disjunct silently carried\nthe requirement the frame was watching call_construct for).\n\nTWO ROUTES BREAK L3/L4, AND ONLY ONE OF THEM IS RECEIVER SYNTAX:\n  R1  method-kind row          -> needs RECEIVER TYPING\n  R2  row whose name is in the catalogue's own ambiguous_names\n                               -> needs MODULE CONTEXT\nA DUAL row (method-kind AND ambiguous-named) is fixed by receiver typing alone,\nbecause for a method row the receiver type IS what fills the module slot. The\ngenuinely distinct population is R2-ONLY: function-kind, ambiguous-named, with\nno receiver to type. ~392 rows, and receiver typing cannot reach them at any\nquality.\n\n    lang         rows  R1-only  R2-only   dual  neither   route\n    kotlin        186      143        3     38        2   receiver\n    objc          131      123        0      0        6   receiver  NEVER MEASURED\n    scala         184      112        5     48       15   receiver\n    java          142      104        3     35        0   receiver\n    swift         107       93        0      7        7   receiver\n    python        659      167       84     86      305   both\n    rust          173       61        7     58       46   both (drained)\n    go            281       30       24     35      156   both\n    javascript    314       21       32     10      245   both\n    elixir        529        0      133      0      389   ambiguous-name\n    erlang        278        0       59      0      214   ambiguous-name\n    c             115        0       16      0       83   ambiguous-name  CONFIRMED\n    cpp           121        0       16      0       89   ambiguous-name\n    haskell       276        0       10      0      233   ambiguous-name\n    bash            9        0        0      0        9   neither\n\nDenominator is ALL ROWS; the 2026-08-06 table's was SINKS ONLY, so the\npercentages are not comparable across the two. The finding -- R1 = 0 does not\nimply not-at-risk -- is denominator-independent.\n\nC IS A CONFIRMED L4 FAIL WITH ITS CONTROL IN THE SAME REPO. On qemu-dtc (a\nSECOND C repo, independent of WI-lajus's socket corpus), same analyzer, same\nrun: fopen/fread/fwrite (not ambiguous, 7/6/2 sites) REACH their rows, while\nopen/write/close/read (ambiguous, 6/6/5/1 sites) reach nothing. The only\nproperty separating them is ambiguous-name membership.\n\nTHE OTHER FOUR R2 LANGUAGES ARE \"NOT BLIND\", WHICH IS WEAKER THAN A PASS:\nelixir reaches 12/134 R2 rows on phoenix-framework, erlang 14/60 on rebar3,\nhaskell 1/10 on servant, cpp 4/22 on modsecurity -- module context does arrive\nat the call site at least sometimes. NOT-REACHED IS NOT A FAILURE COUNT: a row\ncan be unreached because the repo never calls it.\n\nANALYZER STATUS AT L2, from WI-nasuf's fixture measurement (2026-08-05, real\npipeline + production _match_propagation_entry, in-fixture positive control):\n\n    java, scala, swift    emit and match in BOTH forms      OK\n    python                two-step emits; chained NO EDGE   WI-makij / INV-mumov\n    kotlin, javascript    NO EDGE in either form            WI-nasuf\n    objc                  NOT MEASURED                      <- STILL OPEN 2026-09-09\n    go, rust              measured and DRAINED since        WI-doluf/WI-vadin/WI-hikug;\n                                                            INV-pamis/WI-dizag/WI-lalot\n\nOBJC IS NOW THE CELL THAT MATTERS MOST, and it is not the C route: 123 of its\n131 rows are method-kind (94%), the third-highest R1 exposure of any language,\nand as of 2026-09-09 it has NO filed item and NO corpus measurement anywhere.\nBehind it, java/scala/swift rest on FIXTURE evidence only (WI-nasuf,\n2026-08-05) at 90-100% combined exposure.\n\nL1 IS A SEPARATE AXIS AND COMES AFTER, NOT BEFORE. 11 of 15 io_primitives\ncatalogues are status: in_progress (python, rust, erlang and javascript are\nprovenance_declared as of 2026-09-09),\nincluding go and javascript. Completeness and reachability MULTIPLY: adding\nmethod-kind entries to kotlin's catalogue returns exactly zero while 0% of\nkotlin method calls emit an edge. So L2/L3 is sequenced first and L1 follows.\n\nWHAT CLOSES THIS ITEM: a per-language table of the full L2->L4 chain for all\nnine at-risk languages, the three unmeasured cells filled, and the declining\nbranch READ (not inferred) in each failing analyzer, so that the fix can be\nsorted by shape per LIVE.md rule 5 -- one chokepoint if the branches agree, per\nanalyzer if they spread. Only kotlin's branch has been read so far (emission\ngated on resolver.lookup(...).found, which a JDK method can never satisfy).\n\nDIRECTION DISCIPLINE. Every fix under this class must state which direction it\nmoves the taint walk's False verdict. More call edges feed more callees_at,\nwhich feeds section 7a sanitizer registration, so an emission fix CAN\nmanufacture suppression (False earns `sanitized`, #214, and a sanitized flow is\ndropped). Measure it; do not assume the change is additive because the defect\nis a false negative."  # ce65
- op: discuss  # 9982
  at: '2026-09-09T16:57:30Z'  # 9982
  by: agent  # 9982
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 9982
  clock: 77  # 9982
  nonce: '9982'  # 9982
  message: "OPTION C TAKEN, owner ruling 2026-09-09: KEEP THE STATEMENT, REPAIR THE FRAME.\nConsidered against A (widen the class to a second mechanism) and B (re-scope\nthis item to receiver syntax and split the ambiguous-name route into its own\nclass). C was chosen; recording what it means so nobody re-litigates it.\n\nWHAT CHANGED. The description's exposure table was one-dimensional (method-kind\nshare) and could not predict this class's own counterexamples. It has been\nREPLACED, not annotated, with a mechanism x language table -- R1-only /\nR2-only / dual / neither, all 15 shipped catalogues. The falsified sentence is\nexcised from the table and survives only inside the retraction that names it.\n\nWHAT DID NOT CHANGE. The statement is untouched and still quantifies over every\nlanguage. The class still covers both routes. C is explicitly NOT a narrowing:\nthe diagnosis was that the FRAME was one-dimensional, not that the boundary was\ndrawn in the wrong place.\n\nWHY NOT B, since I recommended it. The dual-row finding weakened the clean-\npartition argument -- 7 of 15 languages carry both routes and 317 rows are\nmethod-kind AND ambiguous-named, so a split would have put both classes across\nthe same languages. And re-scoping a P1 immediately after measuring it is\ngoalpost-moving unless the evidence forces it; it did not force it.\n\nTHE THREE CELLS PHASE 1 EXPOSED ARE NOW FILED, all P2 todo_hard, all SIZING\nitems rather than fix items (INV-linub's own go precedent, 2026-08-10: \"SIZE IT\nBEFORE BUILDING IT\", and INV-tanom is the cautionary case for assuming a cell is\ncostly):\n\n  WI-robit  objc -- 123/131 rows method-kind, 3rd-highest R1 exposure, NO item\n            and NO corpus measurement anywhere. A PURE receiver-syntax cell\n            (R2-only = 0), so it is this class's original mechanism undiluted.\n            Blocked on writing an objc emitter for measure-catalogue-reach.py.\n  WI-fasoz  java/scala/swift -- the \"OK in both forms\" verdict is FIXTURE\n            evidence only (WI-nasuf 2026-08-05) at 90-100% combined exposure.\n            Confirm or refute on corpus. Closure-evidence discipline requires\n            this before those cells can be satisfied anyway.\n  WI-kurug  cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell -- 218 R2-only rows, status \"not blind\",\n            which is neither a pass nor a failure. Apply the qemu-dtc\n            within-repo contrast to four more languages. Likely outcome is a\n            dated positive declaration, not a fix.\n\nC's cell stays with WI-lajus and is now CONFIRMED FAILING on a second repo.\n\nWHAT CLOSES THIS ITEM, restated against the repaired frame: every cell of the\nmechanism x language table reads PASSES with behavioural evidence, FAILS with a\nfiled item, or is a dated declared blindness -- and then ONE class-level\nre-measurement through the taint path, which has never been run. Per-instance\nPRs are not that measurement.\n\nINSTRUMENT DEBT, disclosed: census.py and analyze.py live in the lab notebook\nand are NOT in-repo or tested, so their defects can only be re-encountered\n(LIVE.md rule 14). census.py is the one worth landing -- it reads production's\nown load_catalog and would make this table regenerable instead of a snapshot\nthat starts rotting today."  # 9982
