 0   both\n    swift          107       93        0     7        7   receiver\n    rust           173       61        7    59       46   both\n    go             281       45       24    40      172   both\n    javascript     314       21       34    10      249   both\n    elixir         529        0      134     0      395   ambiguous-name\n    erlang         278        0       60     0      218   ambiguous-name\n    c              115        0       22     0       93   ambiguous-name\n    cpp            121        0       22     0       99   ambiguous-name\n    haskell        276        0       10     0      266   ambiguous-name\n    bash             9        0        0     0        9   neither\n\nREGENERATE THIS TABLE, DO NOT RESTATE IT: it is the verbatim output of\n`python3 scripts/measure-catalogue-exposure.py` (landed 2026-09-09, PR\n6b72c22384). The 2026-09-09 revision of this block was pasted from the lab\nprototype, which deduplicated by qualified_name where the shipped script\ncounts RAW ROWS -- c read 16 not 22, go's R1 split 30/35 not 45/40, and\nkotlin/scala/java read route `receiver` not `both`. No verdict moved, but a\ntable disagreeing with the tool that regenerates it is the drift the tool\nexists to stop. Row counts are the unit here; the script also reports\ndistinct qualified names.\n\nDenominator is ALL ROWS; the 2026-08-06 table's was SINKS ONLY, so the\npercentages are not comparable across the two. The finding -- R1 = 0 does not\nimply not-at-risk -- is denominator-independent.\n\nC IS A CONFIRMED L4 FAIL WITH ITS CONTROL IN THE SAME REPO. On qemu-dtc (a\nSECOND C repo, independent of WI-lajus's socket corpus), same analyzer, same\nrun: fopen/fread/fwrite (not ambiguous, 7/6/2 sites) REACH their rows, while\nopen/write/close/read (ambiguous, 6/6/5/1 sites) reach nothing. The only\nproperty separating them is ambiguous-name membership.\n\nTHE OTHER FOUR R2 LANGUAGES ARE \"NOT BLIND\", WHICH IS WEAKER THAN A PASS:\nelixir reaches 12/134 R2 rows on phoenix-framework, erlang 14/60 on rebar3,\nhaskell 1/10 on servant, cpp 4/22 on modsecurity -- module context does arrive\nat the call site at least sometimes. NOT-REACHED IS NOT A FAILURE COUNT: a row\ncan be unreached because the repo never calls it.\n\nANALYZER STATUS AT L2, from WI-nasuf's fixture measurement (2026-08-05, real\npipeline + production _match_propagation_entry, in-fixture positive control):\n\n    java, scala, swift    emit and match in BOTH forms      OK\n    python                two-step emits; chained NO EDGE   WI-makij / INV-mumov\n    kotlin, javascript    NO EDGE in either form            WI-nasuf\n    objc                  EMITS, and types 50.2% of        MEASURED 2026-09-09 by\n                          external method receivers        WI-robit -- the NO EDGE\n                                                           row below is STALE.\n                                                           Residual: WI-garar\n    go, rust              measured and DRAINED since        WI-doluf/WI-vadin/WI-hikug;\n                                                            INV-pamis/WI-dizag/WI-lalot\n\nOBJC IS NOW THE CELL THAT MATTERS MOST, and it is not the C route: 123 of its\n131 rows are method-kind (94%), the third-highest R1 exposure of any language,\nand as of 2026-09-09 it has NO filed item and NO corpus measurement anywhere.\nBehind it, java/scala/swift rest on FIXTURE evidence only (WI-nasuf,\n2026-08-05) at 90-100% combined exposure.\n\nL1 IS A SEPARATE AXIS AND COMES AFTER, NOT BEFORE. 11 of 15 io_primitives\ncatalogues are status: in_progress (python, rust, erlang and javascript are\nprovenance_declared as of 2026-09-09),\nincluding go and javascript. Completeness and reachability MULTIPLY: adding\nmethod-kind entries to kotlin's catalogue returns exactly zero while 0% of\nkotlin method calls emit an edge. So L2/L3 is sequenced first and L1 follows.\n\nWHAT CLOSES THIS ITEM: a per-language table of the full L2->L4 chain for all\nnine at-risk languages, the three unmeasured cells filled, and the declining\nbranch READ (not inferred) in each failing analyzer, so that the fix can be\nsorted by shape per LIVE.md rule 5 -- one chokepoint if the branches agree, per\nanalyzer if they spread. Only kotlin's branch has been read so far (emission\ngated on resolver.lookup(...).found, which a JDK method can never satisfy).\n\nDIRECTION DISCIPLINE. Every fix under this class must state which direction it\nmoves the taint walk's False verdict. More call edges feed more callees_at,\nwhich feeds section 7a sanitizer registration, so an emission fix CAN\nmanufacture suppression (False earns `sanitized`, #214, and a sanitized flow is\ndropped). Measure it; do not assume the change is additive because the defect\nis a false negative."  # 0041
- op: discuss  # cb69
  at: '2026-09-09T18:13:07Z'  # cb69
  by: agent  # cb69
  actor: jgstern_agent  # cb69
  clock: 81  # cb69
  nonce: cb69  # cb69
  message: "OBJC CELL MEASURED 2026-09-09 (WI-robit) -- THIS ITEM'S OWN STATUS FOR OBJC WAS WRONG, and the description is corrected rather than annotated.\n\nIt recorded objc as `NOT MEASURED` with a blind-language row of `NO EDGE / emit,NOMATCH(method)`. Measured on four repos (AFNetworking, CocoaLumberjack, fmdb, Mantle): objc EMITS the call edge, STAMPS call_construct=\"method\", and TYPES 50.2% of its external method receivers -- 2,308 typed against 2,290 carrying the `external` sentinel. It reaches 27 of its 125 method-kind rows (21.6%).\n\nSo objc was never the largest hole in the way the phase-1 table implied. It is a HALF-WORKING cell with a named residual, not a blind one. My own pre-registered prediction (<10% reach) was refuted too.\n\nTHE RESIDUAL IS A PRECEDENTED SHAPE, filed as WI-garar: objc types locals whose type is written and class-name receivers, but never a `self.<property>` receiver. The contrast is inside one corpus and one run -- `[handle writeData:data]` on `NSFileHandle *handle` reaches its row, `[self.session dataTaskWithRequest:request]` on `@property NSURLSession *session` reaches nothing. Rust closed exactly this as WI-dizag shape A and python worked it twice (INV-mumov, WI-zamud). Consequence on real code: AFNetworking, an HTTP client, reaches net_send and ZERO net_recv.\n\nTWO NULLS THAT ARE CORRECT, recorded so nobody re-files them as defects: fmdb reaches zero db rows because objc's db rows are all Core Data and fmdb is a SQLite wrapper over the raw sqlite3_* C API; and AFNetworking's 50 source hits for dataTaskWithRequest are not 50 missed sites, since 13 of 15 task-creation edges resolve IN-REPO to AFNetworking's own same-named wrapper.\n\nWHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE PHASE-1 UNEXAMINED SET: objc is now examined and its cell reads EMITS + 50.2% typed + WI-garar. Remaining unexamined: java/scala/swift (WI-fasoz, fixture evidence only) and cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell (WI-kurug, the R2 route). The C cell is CONFIRMED and now FIXED at L3/L4 by WI-lajus, with its L1 half filed as WI-hasul and WI-bulub."  # cb69
- op: discuss  # bd52
  at: '2026-09-09T18:52:49Z'  # bd52
  by: agent  # bd52
  actor: jgstern_agent  # bd52
  clock: 82  # bd52
  nonce: bd52  # bd52
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-09-09: \"closing INV-linub should be integrated into the front\nof the rest of the arc.\" Recorded in arc_finishing_order_09062026.md as T0,\nrunning BEFORE INV-busis and before T3 WI-vodin; T3-T9 keep their order behind\nit. This is a SCHEDULING ruling; it does not change the statement, the frame,\nor any verdict.\n\nWHAT THIS ITEM'S CLOSURE ACTUALLY REQUIRES, since the question has now been\nasked four times and the item answers it in two places that disagree.\n\nThe WHAT-CLOSES clause in the description is a MEASUREMENT clause and two of\nits three conjuncts are discharged:\n  (1) the declining branch READ in each failing analyzer, sorted by shape --\n      ANSWERED, and answered NO: the branches do not share one shape (python\n      one, go the mirror, rust a third). Recorded 2026-09-09.\n  (2) the per-language exposure table -- REBUILT and now REGENERABLE rather\n      than restated (scripts/measure-catalogue-exposure.py, PR 6b72c22384).\n  (3) the unmeasured cells -- objc filled by WI-robit; java/scala/swift\n      (WI-fasoz) and cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell (WI-kurug) remain.\n\nBut this is an INVARIANT and its statement quantifies over EVERY language.\nMarking it satisfied because the measurement finished would substitute a\nPROCESS PROXY for behavioural evidence -- the discipline INV-nufob was\nfalse-satisfied against. So the 2026-09-09 ledger entry's formulation stands\nand is the operative one: IT CLOSES WHEN THE LANGUAGES DRAIN.\n\nTHE POINT OF T0 IS THAT THE DRAIN IS BOUNDED. Five live in-class\ncounterexamples: INV-mumov (python, violated), WI-garar (objc), WI-dosuh /\nWI-vihop / WI-kikar (javascript). Best case five items, worst case nine.\n\nWI-fasoz and WI-kurug GO FIRST INSIDE T0 because they are the only two items\nthat can change the LENGTH of that list -- both are sizing items and can only\nADD (0-3 and 0-4 respectively). A drain cannot be scheduled before it is\ncounted.\n\nEXPLICITLY NOT IN THE DRAIN, so they are not re-added later:\n  WI-hasul / WI-bulub  C L1 completeness. THIS ITEM sequences L1 as a separate\n                       axis that comes AFTER L2/L3, in its own words.\n  INV-tanom            never this item's go cell (reframed 2026-09-09); the\n                       statement is disjunctive and go satisfies it via the\n                       import block. Stays violated as an axis defect.\n  WI-difup             haskell L1 function-kind; stripped as never an instance.\n\nCONSEQUENCE FOR THE INV-busis ORDERING COLLISION: it is no longer the front and\nis now due at the END of T0 rather than immediately. Still unruled."  # bd52
- op: discuss  # f003
  at: '2026-09-09T19:17:16Z'  # f003
  by: agent  # f003
  actor: jgstern_agent  # f003
  clock: 83  # f003
  nonce: f003  # f003
  message: "T0a COMPLETE 2026-09-09 -- THE DRAIN IS COUNTED, AND IT IS TEN, NOT FIVE.\nRecords: ~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/t0_sizing_09092026/ (one pre-registration\ncovering both items, written before either number existed; kurug/ and fasoz/\nhold the instruments, adjudications and results).\n\nWI-kurug and WI-fasoz are both DONE. They were the only two items that could\nchange the LENGTH of this item's drain list, which is why the owner's\nfront-of-arc ruling put them first. My pre-registered net prediction was \"T0b\nstays at five, plus 1-2 java/scala residuals\" -- six or seven. **The actual\ncount is ten. Prediction refuted, in the direction of more work.**\n\nTHE DRAIN, as of now:\n    python       INV-mumov   violated -- attribute-chain receivers\n    objc         WI-garar    self.<property> never typed\n    javascript   WI-dosuh / WI-vihop / WI-kikar\n    scala        WI-sigog    NEW, P1 -- see below\n    swift        WI-dodop    NEW -- 16.5% typed\n    elixir       WI-kafor    NEW -- module-qualified call binds by bare name\n    elixir       WI-kigub    NEW -- atom-module call emits no edge\n    haskell      WI-lokun    NEW -- Prelude.print, the one true R2 failure\n\nTWO FINDINGS THAT CHANGE THIS ITEM'S OWN RECORDED CONTENT.\n\n1. **THE ANALYZER STATUS BLOCK'S \"java, scala, swift -- emit and match in BOTH\n   forms OK\" IS REFUTED FOR SCALA.** On corpus: **0 of 40,970 external\n   method-call edges carry a receiver type**, across sbt (36 MB) and lila\n   (185 MB), with 0/164 method rows reached through scala's own edges. Boundary\n   chains by edge language read bash/java/c on sbt and typescript/javascript/\n   bash/python on lila -- scala contributes 0 and 1 respectively. scala emits\n   all 40,970 calls stamped `call_construct: \"method\"` and never types the\n   receiver. 90% of scala's rows are at risk and it has NO second disjunct.\n   That verdict rested entirely on WI-nasuf's 2026-08-05 FIXTURE evidence,\n   which is exactly the thinness WI-fasoz was filed to test. java, measured on\n   the same instrument the same day, came back 65.0% -- so the instrument is\n   not what produces the zero. java CONFIRMED (65.0%, above objc's 50.2%);\n   swift PASSES WEAKLY (16.5%, mechanism works, inferred receivers missed).\n\n2. **THE R2 (AMBIGUOUS-NAME) ROUTE DOES NOT BLIND cpp/elixir/erlang/haskell.**\n   erlang 14/14, cpp 2/3 (and its one miss is a DUPLICATE catalogue row at a\n   call site that does reach), haskell 1 genuine failure, elixir 9 misses none\n   of which are this route. erlang and cpp pass via the module_hint disjunct --\n   the same unanticipated-route pattern as go (INV-tanom). So phase 1's\n   correction stands (R1=0 does not imply not-at-risk) but the follow-through\n   is narrower than the 218-row exposure suggested: exposure is an UPPER BOUND\n   and this is what the bound is worth when you check which rows are actually\n   called, in files the analyzer actually read.\n\nMETHOD NOTE FOR WHOEVER MEASURES A CELL NEXT -- three traps, all of which\nchanged an answer here before being caught by reading rows back:\n  * `primitives_used` is a REPO-WIDE roll-up and sibling catalogues SHARE rows.\n    scala was credited with java's reach inside sbt (14 -> 0 once attributed\n    per chain's `io_edge_dst` language). Attribute per language or the number\n    flatters.\n  * A row can be called in a file the analyzer NEVER OPENED (rebar3 vendors\n    under `vendor/`; 0 edges from there; all six erlang \"misses\" were vendored,\n    6 -> 0). This is one layer beyond this item's own uncalled-row warning.\n  * Bare-name matching against the reach set is the very conflation F3 exists\n    to prevent, and sbt's reach set contains binary control-character blobs and\n    an empty string (WI-fisoh).\n  The instrument was CALIBRATED on C/qemu-dtc first, where the answer is known\n  in both directions (0/4 pre-WI-lajus, 3/4 post), and reproduced that signed\n  movement unchanged through two rounds of tightening.\n\nALSO FILED, NOT part of the drain because neither is a receiver-evidence\nfailure: WI-fisoh (io-boundaries reports binary blobs and an empty string as\nprimitive names) and WI-fovub (cpp/elixir duplicate and mis-homed catalogue\nrows -- `stat` under both `unistd` and `sys/stat`).\n\nWHAT REMAINS BEFORE THIS ITEM CAN BE SATISFIED: the ten above, drained with\nper-language behavioural evidence. The measurement half of its WHAT-CLOSES\nclause is now fully discharged -- the chokepoint question was answered NO, the\nexposure table is regenerable, and every cell that rested on fixture evidence\nhas been taken to corpus."  # f003
- op: discuss  # 648c
