  clock: 84  # 648c
  nonce: 648c  # 648c
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-09-09, ask 3 of 3 on the INV-busis thread. Recorded here because\nT0c is this item and the ruling binds how T0c is measured.\n\nONE declared-sanitizer suppression arm at T0c, over the DRAINED SET — not one per\ndrain cell.\n\nWHAT IT SUPERSEDES. INV-busis's 2026-09-07 entry concluded: \"escape-closing work\n(this item's 4a bucket, INV-mumov, INV-linub) is NOT free. Its only reachable effect\non findings today is to delete them ... It should not ship without its own\ndeclared-sanitizer suppression arm.\" Read per-cell that is nine arms across the nine\nopen cells. It was an AGENT recommendation, never ruled, and the owner has now ruled\nit down to one: the arm measures a family-level property of the SANITIZER CATALOGUE,\nnot of any individual cell's receiver typing, so nine repetitions would re-measure\nthe same effect nine times.\n\nWHAT THE ARM IS. The INV-busis 2026-09-07 shape, run once over whatever the drain has\nproduced by the time T0c is reached: scripts/measure-escape-closing-ceiling.py, arm A\nproduction vs arm B (_use_site_terminates -> True, every escape closed at once),\nclaims = the seven generic claims VERBATIM plus an `extra_catalogs: sanitizers:`\nblock. THE KEY IS `sanitizers:` AND NOT `taint_sanitizers:` — the latter is the name\nthe CLI help uses for the FLAGS, the block is silently dropped, and the run prints a\nconfident zero. Carry the pre-registered control that caught it: barrier_walks MUST\nbe > 0 in the declared arm, or the declaration did not reach the arm.\n\nWHAT IT IS FOR. Not to justify the drain — the drain is receiver typing and its own\nper-cell measurements price it. It is to establish, once, what the drained set does\nto the SUPPRESSION direction, so T0c's closure states it rather than leaving it to be\ndiscovered later.\n\nRELATED, SAME RULING: ADR-0052 retires ADR-0017 §3a's removal-coverage ambition, so\nthe escape population these cells sit in is now a declared limitation. That does NOT\nweaken this item — INV-linub is about a catalogued call site REACHING its catalogue\nentry (emission + receiver evidence), which is recall, and is untouched by a decision\nabout refutation."  # 648c
- op: discuss  # 7cc4
  at: '2026-09-10T18:30:36Z'  # 7cc4
  by: agent  # 7cc4
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 7cc4
  clock: 85  # 7cc4
  nonce: 7cc4  # 7cc4
  message: "OWNER RULING 2026-09-10 — WHAT DRAINS A CELL, AND WHAT THIS LEDGER STILL OWES.\nThis is the clause T0c must read; it changes both halves of the closing condition.\n\nTHE QUESTION. Nothing in the record said what makes a T0b cell done. Two bars were\ndefensible: (a) the fix is correct, loses nothing, and its newly-resolved rows have\nbeen read back against source; or (b) all of that PLUS a demonstration that a\nuser-visible answer moved — a verdict flipping in verify-claims, or an I/O primitive\nnewly reached. INV-mumov forced the question by landing exactly between them: +2,943\ncorrectly-resolved ORM-slot edges, 0 lost, ablation 78.9 against a pre-fixed kill-10,\nboth controls moved 0 — and NO verdict changed and NO primitive newly reached.\n\nTHE ANSWER IS (c), SPLIT ACROSS TWO LEVELS.\n\n  1. A CELL DRAINS ON CORRECTNESS. Right, zero losses, rows read back against\n     source. No per-cell primitive delta is required. A SHIPPED, VERIFIED CELL NOW\n     CLOSES ITS ROW HERE — which SUPERSEDES the standing note \"no shipped cell\n     satisfies its INV-linub row yet\" for the correctness half.\n\n  2. THIS LEDGER CARRIES THE PRIMITIVE DEMAND. INV-linub is NOT satisfied by ten\n     correct cells alone. T0c must show ONCE, over the DRAINED SET, that the drain\n     moved user-visible answers — primitives newly reached and/or verdicts moved.\n\nWHY IT WAS SPLIT RATHER THAN PICKED. Per-cell (b) would have made the bar un-meetable\nfor any language with no chokepoint where a resolution gain converts into a boundary\nfinding. Python had Django; haskell and elixir may have nothing equivalent. Those\ncells would not have been slow — they would have been UNDRAINABLE AS FILED and would\nhave needed re-scoping on a technicality, which is the failure mode \"RE-SCOPE, DON'T\nCLOSE\" exists to prevent, arrived at from the wrong direction. Putting the demand on\nthe ledger keeps it honest (a drain that moves no answer is not worth ten cells)\nwithout making each language re-litigate it.\n\nT0c THEREFORE HAS TWO ARMS OVER THE DRAINED SET, BOTH SINGLE, NEITHER PER-CELL:\n  - the declared-sanitizer suppression arm (owner 2026-09-09, already recorded here);\n  - the primitive-delta demonstration (this ruling).\n\nCLOSURE-EVIDENCE NOTE. This does NOT weaken the discipline. Both halves remain\nbehavioural: per-cell correctness is adjudicated against source, and the ledger half\nis a live measurement over the drained set. What is barred is exactly what was barred\nbefore — satisfying this item on \"measurement complete\" (the INV-nufob shape).\n\nDRAIN STATE at this ruling: 10 cells; 4 merged (scala WI-sigog + WI-pokam, objc\nWI-garar, swift WI-dodop, python INV-mumov), 1 committed and awaiting its PR\n(javascript WI-vihop), 5 left — javascript WI-dosuh / WI-kikar, elixir WI-kafor /\nWI-kigub (grammar NOT installed locally), haskell WI-lokun.\n\nRecorded in arc_finishing_order_09062026.md and LIVE.md §3."  # 7cc4
- op: discuss  # 8222
  at: '2026-09-10T20:29:52Z'  # 8222
  by: agent  # 8222
  actor: jgstern_agent  # 8222
  clock: 86  # 8222
  nonce: '8222'  # 8222
  message: "LEDGER EVIDENCE (Q2) FROM THE PYTHON CELL — a table, per the owner's 2026-09-10\nruling, NOT a verdict, and NOT a discharge of this ledger's demand.\n\nUnder ruling (c) a cell drains on correctness while THIS ledger owes ONE\ndemonstration, over the DRAINED SET, that the drain moves user-visible answers.\nINV-mumov's directed DEEP A/B (record:\n~/hypergumbo_lab_notebook/mumov_ab_09102026/PREREGISTRATION.md + addendum) is one\ncell's contribution to that demand. It is filed here so T0c does not have to\nre-derive it, and it must NOT be read as T0c being done — one cell is not the set.\n\nMETHOD: cohort pretix + django, one session and one XDG_CACHE_HOME per arm, py.py\nblob asserted at BEGIN and END. ON = dev tip; OFF = 7d8b675c4d. Both arms 2 GOOD.\n\nWHAT MOVED. Every DEEP band metric on pretix is flat to four decimals except one:\n\n    metric                     pretix OFF   pretix ON      delta   django delta\n    io_tag_rate                    1.7519      2.2962   +31.1%        +0.3%\n    io_boundary_types_found            16          16        0            0\n    avg_slice_nodes               369.889     370.000   +0.111        0.000\n    slice_coverage_pct             0.8910      0.8913  +0.0002       0.0000\n    centrality_gini                0.6654      0.6656  +0.0003      -0.0000\n    tier1_pct                     84.8939     84.8918  -0.0020       0.0000\n    dataflow_slice_ratio           0.3392      0.3382  -0.0009       0.0000\n    access_mode_coverage          38.2658     38.3216  +0.0558      -0.0026\n\nHOW TO READ IT, stated rather than left to inference:\n\n1. A user-visible output DID move. `io_tag_rate` — the share of the graph carrying\n   I/O boundary information — rose 31.1% on the Django APPLICATION and 0.3% on the\n   FRAMEWORK, tracking the same 18.6:1 differential the edge counts show. So the\n   answer to \"does a correctness-only drain reach anything a developer reads\" is\n   not automatically no.\n\n2. **But no new KIND of boundary was reached.** `io_boundary_types_found` is 16 in\n   BOTH arms. More sites tagged, no new primitive type. That is the same shape as\n   the pre-merge four-arm result (\"no verdict changed, no new primitive reached\"),\n   arrived at on a DIFFERENT instrument, and it should be read as CORROBORATION of\n   that finding rather than as a new one.\n\n3. So this cell supports a WEAKER claim than the ledger needs. It shows recall\n   reaching the I/O tagging surface; it does NOT show a primitive newly reached or\n   a verdict moved. If the other cells behave the same way, T0c will have to\n   decide whether \"more sites tagged, no new type\" satisfies the ledger's demand —\n   and that question belongs to T0c and the owner, not to this entry.\n\nLIMIT: no arm was run twice, so sub-1% movements here are not distinguishable from\nrun-to-run variation. The 31.1% is far outside that; nothing else in the table is\nrelied upon. n_effective = 1 per repo."  # 8222
